{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CDDqpMS75P6JnG9jpHAhET","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tCreated-Local: 2026-10-13 17:40 +07\n2\t# Task: assess snapshots of the semantic-register VM at ANY pc (Architect; design note only)\n3\tYou are claude-fable-5-1, NEW session. Worktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7plan` (branch `srvm-phase7-plan`, from master 781a9ba8: phases 1-6 merged). Edit ONLY your report `collab/1792430000000-architect-any-pc-snapshot.report.md`. No code, no docs. Vocabulary per UCF 7.5.3 (cursor, cursor cell, cursor-ref, cell).\n4\tOwner's argument (2026-10-13), which you must test, not assume: (1) \"Taking a snapshot of the semantic VM can be lowered to the register VM and the stack VM at any point in time\"; (2) \"IO is modeled as a monad, so it is pure computation\": a VM step is a pure transition even around effects, an effect is a pending entry (value) in the state and performing it is the driver's job (custody, fencing, op-ids, ledger), so a state between operand fetch and an effect has no half-issued effect; (3) therefore safe points (phase 6's restriction that `lift-state`/`lift-task` refuse any pc whose predecessor is not a parking instruction, and the validator's window rules at parking points) are a scoping choice, not a necessity. Our current understanding (the orchestrator conceded this): the static correspondence exists at every pc (`layout.cljc` `:pairs`, `:a->h`, `:h->a`, `phys`/`virt`, `live-out`); at any pc the stack depth and pending operands are determined by the structure, the live registers by liveness, and linear-scan allocation never lets two live virtuals share a physical register at one pc; what is restricted is the dynamic half.\n5\tRead: `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md` (sections 3 safepoints, 4 layouts, 5.2 rows, 5.6 laws, 7.1-7.2, 8.3, 11, and every \"Amended after 6b/6c\" clause), `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc`, `derive.cljc` (register image and liveness), `frame.cljc` (the validator), `rows.cljc` (`lift-task`/`lower-task`), the semantic-register kernel `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register.cljc` (how a step parks, the ready queue, wait-set, `register-restore`), the stack kernel `debruijn/stack.cljc` (tail seam) and register kernel `debruijn/register.cljc` read only, and the harness `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc`. The old stack/register kernels die at phase 8: say what part of the value survives that.\n6\tAnswer, with evidence from the code: (A) Is the owner right: is every instruction boundary of the semantic-register VM a state the carrier could express canonically, for the semantic-register kernel alone, and for cross-engine lowering (laws 4 and 5)? Walk the instruction set (`:call`, `:ffi-call`, `:stream-put`, `:next`, `:tail`, `:return`, branches, closures, `:act` vs `:tail` frames, install children) and list, per instruction class, the state shapes between operand fetch and the step, what the layout would be, and any obstruction. (B) The two remaining questions: the ready-queue \"not quiescent\" rule (what is the true reason: one task per carrier, scheduler state, a pending wake?) and anything else that is genuinely about the OUTSIDE world (op-ids, custody) at a non-parked pc. (C) What must change: the validator rules (window membership at an arbitrary pc), `lift-state`/`lower-state` refusals, the rows (does an `:act` row carry a pc already; is there a new row kind), the tail-completion seam, the harness laws (what is the matrix at every pc; sampling strategy: every pc of the corpus is how many cases), and the numbering/carrier (does anything depend on parking). (D) Value and cost: what any-pc snapshots buy (time travel, debugging, replay speed, checkpoint at fuel exhaustion, preemption, migration of a running task) versus replay from the last safe point plus a step count (\"derive, don't persist\"); an honest estimate of the slice in opus days plus review rounds (we average 2-4 codex rounds per slice), the order relative to phase 7 and phase 8 (should it come before the cutover so the old engines can serve as cross-check, or after, with the semantic-register kernel alone), and the risks. (E) A recommendation: do it, defer it, or never; if do it, the slice plan (files, acceptance tests named, hosts) in the same shape as the phase 7 plan. Also flag any wording in the owner's argument you disagree with, and give the exact amendment text for the design if you recommend adopting the generalization. Foreground only. Report starts with a 15-line plain summary for the owner and ends with READY or NOT READY (with blocking questions).\n7\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f7716a8d-bcb9-4b49-9ad0-ed6a5daed388","uuid":"8fe15bbe-fc58-4bf1-9515-6c2784d428f5","timestamp":"2026-10-13T09:46:56.763Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7plan/collab/1792430000000-architect-any-pc-snapshot.prompt.md","content":"Created-Local: 2026-10-13 17:40 +07\n# Task: assess snapshots of the semantic-register VM at ANY pc (Architect; design note only)\nYou are claude-fable-5-1, NEW session. Worktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7plan` (branch `srvm-phase7-plan`, from master 781a9ba8: phases 1-6 merged). Edit ONLY your report `collab/1792430000000-architect-any-pc-snapshot.report.md`. No code, no docs. Vocabulary per UCF 7.5.3 (cursor, cursor cell, cursor-ref, cell).\nOwner's argument (2026-10-13), which you must test, not assume: (1) \"Taking a snapshot of the semantic VM can be lowered to the register VM and the stack VM at any point in time\"; (2) \"IO is modeled as a monad, so it is pure computation\": a VM step is a pure transition even around effects, an effect is a pending entry (value) in the state and performing it is the driver's job (custody, fencing, op-ids, ledger), so a state between operand fetch and an effect has no half-issued effect; (3) therefore safe points