.frame/defect` with its context (`:wait`,\n1848\t`:captured`, `:parked`) and the decoded task context (parked key, live\n1849\tFFI call ids, the root's free environment); a defect is\n1850\t`:yin.k/undecodable` with the rule's kind and the row path (continuation\n1851\t§3). This step runs after canonical numbering (step 6) and before\n1852\tpending validation (step 8).\"\n1853\t\n1854\t**R6a — §8 step 1 `:618-623`.** Replace \"then every profile map against\n1855\tthe registry (only the semantic profile is supported, §2)\" with \"then\n1856\tevery profile map against the one profile map of §2\".\n1857\t\n1858\t**R7 — `:63-68` and §10.4 `:774-777` (ACCEPT).** Delete \"Code stamps\n1859\t`\"v3\"`, `\"b2\"`, `\"r2\"` and the inner version 1 do not move: this is a\n1860\tchange of carrier, not of instruction, scheduler, or value semantics,\n1861\texactly the distinction the amendment draws between its versions and\n1862\texecutable code.\" Replace §10.4 with: \"**Contract stamp.** The code\n1863\tcontract carried is `\"v4\"`, the register-shaped machine's; the carrier\n1864\tversion is 3; the two are independent, and neither names a legacy\n1865\tvalue.\"\n1866\t\n1867\t**R8 — §9 `:737-746` \"Legacy correspondence\" and §10.8 `:797-801`\n1868\t(ACCEPT WITH CHANGES).** Delete both: there is no legacy reader and no\n1869\tnormalisation tool. In §10.3 `:769-773` delete \"Versions 0, 1 and 2\n1870\tremain compatibility inputs. One canonical nested projection of version\n1871\t3 is kept for single-message channels and must stay exactly mutually\n1872\tinverse with the rows.\" and \"Retiring old readers is an owner\n1873\tversion-support decision\", leaving \"Rows are the carrier authority.\"\n1874\tIn §9's law list delete \"Exact mutual inversion is required only between\n1875\tversion 3 and its one canonical nested projection (§10.3)\".\n1876\t\n1877\t**R9 — §10.6 `:782-788`, §10.7 `:792-796`, §2 `:70-81`, §10's\n1878\tproposals list `:821-834`, \"Still open\" `:836-837`, §11 `:839-852`\n1879\t(ACCEPT WITH CHANGES).** §10.6, **whole item**: replace its opening\n1880\t\"**Foreign-engine value profiles.** Required before any implementation\n1881\tbrief claims heterogeneous execution conformance, and not drafted here\"\n1882\twith \"**Foreign-engine value profiles.** Required before an\n1883\timplementation brief claims execution conformance for an engine\n1884\t**outside the three repository lanes** (the semantic-register kernel and\n1885\tthe stack and register kernels, whose reconstruction is the layouts of\n1886\t`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md` §4 and needs no value\n1887\tprofile), and not drafted here\"; keep its list of what such a profile\n1888\tmust cover; and replace \"version 3, like version 2, does\n1889\tnot translate between kernels\" with \"version 3 carries the canonical\n1890\tframe; a stack or register kernel reconstructs it through its layout\n1891\t(continuation §4), which is heterogeneous reconstruction, not\n1892\ttranslation between native formats; foreign-engine *value* profiles for\n1893\tengines outside this repository remain a separate document\". §10.7:\n1894\tdelete (\"there are no other profile tables\"). Proposals list: delete\n1895\t\"semantic-only initial scope\"; add \"one engine-less profile (continuation\n1896\t§2.1)\" and \"the explicit root `free-env` (§6.3)\". \"Still open\": replace\n1897\twith \"none beyond the work-bound limits (§10.10)\". §11: replace \"Code\n1898\tidentity, the contract stamp and profile registry, safepoints,\" with\n1899\t\"Code identity (A), safepoints,\"; delete \"versions 0-2\" references.\n1900\t\n1901\t**R10 — §5.5 `:421-426` custody paragraph.** Replace \"a version-3 fork\n1902\troot is admitted exactly as a version-0 body is, proposes nothing, and\n1903\tis never upgraded to custody\" with \"a fork root proposes nothing and is\n1904\tnever upgraded to custody; an exclusive-only receiver refuses it as\n1905\t`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`\".\n1906\t\n1907\t**R11 — §3 `:115-134`.** Keep verbatim, including \"**body version 3\n1908\tuses BLAKE3 permanently**\" and the per-id algorithm check (the reviewer\n1909\tasked that this explicit rule survive).\n1910\t\n1911\t**U7 — scope and precedence of UCF's historical legacy-body clauses\n1912\t(new).** UCF keeps clauses that describe bodies the row carrier never\n1913\treads: §7.2.1 (versions 0 and 1, `:166-321`), the version-0/1 custody\n1914\tgate and fork rules (`:234-290`), §7.4.3 r5's version-0 notes (`:966-970`:\n1915\t\"A version-0 entry without a phase reads as `:running`\", \"The landed\n1916\tversion-0 reader breaches both\"), §7.7.4's `nil`-header version-0 lift\n1917\tand recovery of legacy fork bytes (`:1781`, `:1806-1807`, `:1844`), and\n1918\t§7.11.1's version-0/1 interoperation rows (`:3051-3057`, `:3095-3096`).\n1919\tInsert at the head of §7.2.1: \"**Scope (2026-10-11).** Body versions 0,\n1920\t1 and 2 and every clause of this document that admits, reads, recovers\n1921\tor interoperates with them are **historical**: they describe the\n1922\tstack-shaped machine's handoff, which leaves at\n1923\t`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md` §10 phase 8. The row carrier of body\n1924\tversion 3 (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md` as amended by\n1925\t`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md`) reads no other version and\n1926\ttakes **precedence** over any sentence below that implies otherwise; a\n1927\tconflict is resolved in its favour without further amendment. **Not\n1928\thistorical**: the custody, fencing and operation-id semantics (§7.7.8,\n1929\t§7.9, §7.4.3's operation ids) travel in version 3 as the root's\n1930\t`custody` slot (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md` §5.5) and stay\n1931\toperative; where those sections say 'a version-1 body' read, for version\n1932\t3, 'a root whose custody policy is `:yin.k/exclusive`', and where they\n1933\tsay 'a version-0 body' or 'fork', 'a root whose policy is `:yin.k/fork`'\n1934\t(§5.5's table). The code stamp `{:yin.code/contract \"v3\" :yin.k/version\n1935\t0}` wherever this document shows it (§7.2's example `:121`, §7.2.1\n1936\t`:183`, §7.3.3 `:232-236`) is the stack-shaped machine's; the\n1937\tregister-shaped machine's contract is `\"v4\"`\n1938\t(`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md` §6), the one the version-3 profile map\n1939\tnames, and §7.2's envelope example is superseded for version 3 by the\n1940\trow carrier's wire unit (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md` §3, ids under\n1941\tJing's default digest).\" The same grep (`legacy`, `version-0`,\n1942\t`version 0`, `versions 0`, `version 1`, `recovery`) found, besides the\n1943\tclauses above: the export-recovery object's version-1 format\n1944\t(`:1789-1807`), which is phase 7's to re-base on the row carrier and is\n1945\tnamed in §8.4 as an entry condition, not amended here; publication\n1946\tfailure recovery (`:1741`), completion recovery (`:2417`) and the\n1947\tledger's \"legacy destructive readers\" (`:2774-2775`), which concern the\n1948\tdriver, the ledger protocol and the ledger reader, not a body version,\n1949\tand are untouched; and the status paragraph `:39-42`, covered by the\n1950\tscope statement's \"every clause\".\n1951\t\n1952\t### 10.4 To code-as-tuples (`yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`) §7.2\n1953\t\n1954\t**C1 (ACCEPT).** Replace the first paragraph with: \"The vector-to-datoms\n1955\tprojection path is **retired** (owner ruling 2026-09-18; frozen §3.4\n1956\t'One vector validator'). There is one load path for a vector, the direct\n1957\tone, and two AST projection lanes (datom lane, row lane) that produce\n1958\tthe vector; all three feed one validator\n1959\t(`yin.vm.semantic-register.code/well-formed?`). UCF §7.3.4's projection\n1960\tpath is superseded accordingly.\" Replace obligation 2's second sentence\n1961\twith \"loading the vector produced by either AST lane and the vector\n1962\tloaded directly yield the same image and address (`load_paths_test.cljc`)\",\n1963\tand obligation 3's \"both paths refuse with the same defect\" with \"the\n1964\tdirect vector load refuses with the published defect (rule and pc) for\n1965\tevery rule of the frozen §3.4\". Obligations 1 and 4 stand.\n1966\t\n1967\t### 10.5 Checked and consistent (no amendment)\n1968\t\n1969\tFrozen §2.4 delivery shapes against the 4b branch; frozen §4.1 two state\n1970\tcolumns; the evaluator design §1.3-1.6; the draft's §3, §4, §5.1 (except\n1971\t`:reified`), §5.3, §6, §7 and §8 steps 1-6, 8-12; v2 amendment §2's `E`\n1972\tshape; the phase-5 §9.2 `:yin.k/trees` text; the register kernel's\n1973\t`:return-result` delivery (`register.cljc:458-468`).\n1974\t\n1975\t### 10.6 Amendments after slice 6b (revision 4, 2026-10-13)\n1976\t\n1977\tEach item names the clause, what changed, and why; the clause itself\n1978\tcarries the same marker. Sources are the engineer report's design bugs\n1979\t(B1-B4) and deviations (1-10) and the three codex reviews' rulings.\n1980\t\n1981\t| # | Clause | What changed | Why |\n1982\t|---|---|---|---|\n1983\t| B1 | §5.2 rows 10-12, the install paragraph; §8.2 item 3 | row 10 is child `:blocked`, phase `:parked`; row 11 is the `:running` wire phase set by hand on row 10's machine; the lower's install contract (phase set, response and image, child `:blocked`) is stated | `advance-install` retains a blocked child at `:parked` and links a halted or parked one in the same sweep; the promised states were unreachable |\n1984\t| B2 | §5.2 wrapper paragraph, row 21 | the mechanical wrapper rule has one stated exception: row 21 under `:depth-3` answers `[3 [2 [1 1]]]` | `nest`'s parameter is `x` and shadows the free `x` before `f` is created; the reference run itself answers 1 |\n1985\t| B3 | §5.2 refusal rows; §8.2 item 7 | the `:tail` fourth slot is `:row-shape` at structure step 2, and the tamper is a raw row edit with rehash | the row table fixes `:tail` at three slots before S1 runs; `emit` drops extra abstract slots silently |\n1986\t| B4 | §7.1 | the lift's order is quiescence, kind, free environment, walk, code, dependency closure, trees, task, frames, emit | segments and contexts are known only after the walk |\n1987\t| D1 | §6.1, §8.2 depends-on | `:source-index` is a lift input from the composition | the kernel holds no source index |\n1988\t| D2 | §7.1 | refusals are thrown `ex-info` | consistency with `decode`/`emit` |\n1989\t| D3 | §2.5, §2.6, §7.2 | custody nil at the root; non-nil is `:yin.k/unsupported :custody` at step 10 | exclusive/holder is phase 7 |\n1990\t| D4 | §2.5, §5.2 row 17 | linked registry entries do not travel; the receiver's registry is a composition prerequisite and is kept as given | no registry slot in the row table |\n1991\t| D5 | §7.1, §7.2 | discharge is per context over live code (`live-pcs`), answered by `E`, the one active store, primitive, installing export, registry; lift only | codex F3 (union over stores was unsound) and F6 (whole-body scan refused valid states) |\n1992\t| D6 | §2.5 | the store slice is the whole root store; every parked record travels; module stores by reachability | conservative and what the code does |\n1993\t| D7 | §6.3 | the retention seam landed; the lift reads the kernel's `free-env` field by default, the option overrides, E2 catches a disagreeing override | 4b stage 3 landed before 6b |\n1994\t| D8 | §7.2 | the lower's four stages, each across the whole tree before the next, all before attachment; the refusal-kind table including `:yin.k/stores` | codex F1, F2, F4, F7, F8: every carrier defect must be a named refusal before any attach |\n1995\t| D9 | §5.2 row 18 | the fixture's module store is symbol-keyed and hand-built | codex asked for the synthetic label |\n1996\t| D10 | §9 | 4b stage 2 landed; rows 3-4, 7-12, 17, 24 run; three-host gate still open | honesty |\n1997\t\n1998\t**Note on `handoff.cljc` (no edit).** The engineer report said\n1999\t`export-task` builds installing names as `(symbol (str m \"/\" x))`, a\n2000\tnil-namespace symbol that never equals `m/x`. Read at\n2001\t`handoff.cljc:1480-1488`, the code is `(symbol (str (name m) \"/\" (name\n2002\tx)))`. The one-argument `symbol` of Clojure, ClojureScript and the\n2003\tpinned ClojureDart core splits its string at `/` into namespace and\n2004\tname, so this yields the qualified `host.mod/f` with namespace\n2005\t`\"host.mod\"`, equal to `'host.mod/f`; codex round 1 evaluated it on the\n2006\tJVM and found the same. The claim is withdrawn; the old form is not a\n2007\tbug. `rows.cljc`'s `installing-names` uses the two-argument `(symbol\n2008\t(name mn) (name x))`, which is equivalent. `handoff.cljc` is not edited\n2009\tby this phase (§8.5).\n2010\t\n2011\t---\n2012\t\n2013\t## 11. Owner questions\n2014\t\n2015\tRevisions 2-3 left none: the reviewer accepted the engine-less profile\n2016\tand rejected the merged-`E` free environment. **Revision 4 (after 6b)\n2017\tlists the design choices the merged code made that no ruling covers.**\n2018\tEach is stated as the code behaves today; none is decided by this\n2019\tdocument.\n2020\t\n2021\t- **Q1, install children, the parked-child scenario.** The engine never\n2022\t  retains an install child at a quiescent safepoint except `:blocked` at\n2023\t  phase `:parked` (§5.2 B1). The carrier still admits phase `:running`\n2024\t  (never engine-reachable at a safepoint) and the lower refuses any\n2025\t  child kind but `:blocked`. Should the row table and both checks\n2026\t  narrow to the one reachable state (phase `:parked`, child `:blocked`),\n2027\t  or does the owner want a reachable parked-child install scenario,\n2028\t  which is an engine change (`advance-install`), with row 11 rewritten\n2029\t  to it?\n2030\t- **Q2, census agreement on lower.** The draft §8 step 9 requires the\n2031\t  carried `requires`/`code` to agree **exactly** with a census recomputed\n2032\t  from the rows (a declared segment not reached, or a reached segment\n2033\t  not declared, is `:yin.k/undecodable`). `check-dependencies!` checks\n2034\t  only that `:yin.k/segments` equals the key set of `code`; a vector\n2035\t  carried but referenced by no row is accepted and attached. Accept the\n2036\t  narrower check, or require the recomputed census?\n2037\t- **Q3, install response verification.** The draft §5.4 asks the\n2038\t  receiver to verify that the install's link id equals `parent`, that\n2039\t  the image hashes to the address it is delivered under and is the image\n2040\t  the manifest names, and that format, contract and the child's profile\n2041\t  agree. `check-install!` verifies status, manifest name, export shape\n2042\t  and that the image loads; not the link id, not the image's address\n2043\t  against the manifest. Accept the narrower contract for phase 6, or\n2044\t  extend it before phase 7 (which re-bases the export-recovery object)?\n2045\t- **Q4, operation ids and correlation on lower.** The draft §8 step 8's\n2046\t  op-id rules (below `next-op-seq`, unique across the tree, absent\n2047\t  without origin) and its correlation agreement (cursor-cell stream,\n2048\t  envelope ids, module names) are not implemented; op-ids travel\n2049\t  verbatim, and pending compatibility is only P2's reason check. With\n2050\t  fork-only custody there is no `next-op-seq`; is this phase 7's with\n2051\t  the custody arm, or a 6-era gap to close?\n2052\t- **Q5, no re-discharge on lower.** Free names are discharged at lift\n2053\t  only (§7.1); the lower checks carried segments, trees, store census,\n2054\t  closures and primitives, and a name a closure resolves through the\n2055\t  registry is a composition prerequisite (§2.5). A receiver lacking that\n2056\t  registry entry accepts the carrier and fails at run time\n2057\t  (`Unable to resolve symbol`). Accept, or require the lower to\n2058\t  re-discharge registry-answered names against the receiver's registry\n2059\t  before attachment?\n2060\t- **Q6, conservatism of the live-code rule.** `live-pcs` follows both\n2061\t  successor edges of every branch and enters every reachable closure\n2062\t  body, with no constant propagation. The harness's `(if false (fn []\n2063\t  ghost) (next-of c))` case transports only because the lift happens at\n2064\t  a safepoint **after** the branch (the resume pc is past it, so the\n2065\t  dead arm is unreachable by edges); the same program lifted at a\n2066\t  safepoint **before** the branch would refuse `ghost` although the arm\n2067\t  can never run. Is \"reachable by control-flow edges, never by value\"\n2068\t  the intended precision, accepting the false refusals it implies for\n2069\t  constant-dead code?\n2070\t- **Q7, host-class primitives behind a shadow.** `:host-state-primitive`\n2071\t  refuses only when the name reaches the primitive step of the answering\n2072\t  order; the same name bound in `E` or the active store lifts (codex\n2073\t  round 2 confirmed). Intended, or should a host-class primitive name\n2074\t  refuse regardless of shadowing?\n2075\t- **Q8, preflight attachment cost.** `attaches?` validates and analyses\n2076\t  every carried vector (and every install image) by attaching it into a\n2077\t  throwaway receiver, and restore attaches it again. Codex found it pure\n2078\t  and only duplicated CPU. Accept, or ask the kernel for a validate-only\n2079\t  entry point?\n2080\t- **Q9, Law 2 outside a fresh receiver.** The id counter is adopted as\n2081\t  `max(local, carried)`; a receiver whose construction mints more ids\n2082\t  than the carrier relifts to a different root (draft §9 already scopes\n2083\t  the law to the fresh-receiver context). No harness case has a\n2084\t  non-fresh receiver. Add one as a documented non-law, or leave it?\n2085\t- **Q10, the `:running` wire phase's future.** If Q1 narrows the phase\n2086\t  set, row 11 disappears from the matrix and the `:install` row's phase\n2087\t  slot becomes a constant; if not, row 11 stays a synthetic case. Which?\n2088\t\n2089\t---\n2090\t\n2091\t## 12. Portability notes for the implementer\n2092\t\n2093\t1. **Nil-valued window keys (Dart, Node).** The window is built with\n2094\t   `select-keys` and encoded as `[r [:lit nil]]`; never filter `some?`\n2095\t   values; `frame-test/nil-valued-register-is-a-member` over row 22\n2096\t   (`(pair nil (next-of c))`, the nil operand evaluated before the read)\n2097\t   is the canary. Membership is always by `contains?`/key sets, never\n2098\t   `(get W r)`.\n2099\t2. **`for` over large seqs (Dart).** Row sets of the C4 modules run to\n2100\t   thousands of rows; build with `mapv`/`into`/`reduce`.\n2101\t3. **Reader whitespace before closers (Dart).** Pinned row sets are\n2102\t   Clojure data in `.cljc`, never EDN strings.\n2103\t4. **Duplicate protocol params (Dart).** `gc-children [_ _x]`\n2104\t   (`srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:386`); any new protocol method\n2105\t   in `layout` likewise.\n2106\t5. **Multi-key `assoc` on nil (Dart).** Frames and windows start from\n2107\t   `{}` (`handoff.cljc:1455-1457` records the trap).\n2108\t6. **Reader conditionals.** `#?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default)`\n2109\t   with `:cljd` first; `:clj`-only source-reading tests as `#?(:cljd nil :clj …)`.\n2110\t7. **Private var access (Dart).** Every function named in §8 is public;\n2111\t   no `#'`.\n2112\t8. **Sorted keys.** Window and bindings rows are sorted by Jing\n2113\t   canonical bytes of the key, never by host `sort` on mixed types.\n2114\t9. **Numeric classes (Node).** `pc`, `rd`, `r`, `site` are exact\n2115\t   integers; compare with `cbor/numeric-kind` before `=` on wire values\n2116\t   (`ucf/profile-engine`, `ucf.cljc:77-91`, is the template).\n2117\t10. **Fresh worktree.** `mise trust` and `npm ci` before the Node lane.\n2118\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f7716a8d-bcb9-4b49-9ad0-ed6a5daed388","uuid":"e5143911-552f-4730-96da-775bbdc0c667","timestamp":"2026-10-13T09:47:18.312Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7plan/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md","content":"  stack slices `[f]` at the inner call of `(f (g x) y)` and at `(g)` in\n  `(f (if c (g) 2) y)` were derived twice from the §4.3 walk and agreed\n  with the reviewer's derivation; the register `phys(1) = 0`, `phys(2) =\n  3` follow from phase-5 §6.3 row 2, whose pinned `live` at pc 3 is `[0]`\n  (`{T0,T3}` minus the destination `T3`), the same set §4.2 step 4 and\n  §5.6 item 3 compute by subtracting the virtual `rd 2` before mapping.\n  The multi-frame bases in 6c item 6 (`2, 4` for the expression alone;\n  `0, 0, 0, 2, 4` as run under the harness's bindings) were derived\n  twice from the kernel's call arm (`stack.cljc:716-733`, base = height\n  after popping callee and operands); the first pass forgot the pushed\n  operator, and revision 2's lowering rule used the height **before**\n  each slice, which the round-2 review caught and §4.3 now states as\n  cumulative through the slice. Nothing was run.\n- **Claims that are checks, not theorems**: the stack operand slice\n  equals the saved set at non-tail safepoints (§5.6 item 5); the non-push\n  H ↔ A correspondence (item 4); `virt` single-valued at reachable\n  boundaries (item 3); layout `E` = evaluator `E` (item 6); the tail\n  lowering's unused fields are unobservable (item 8); the slices\n  partition the stack (item 9).\n- **What is not claimed**: that kernel state round-trips (shadowed outer\n  positional values are nil-filled; the law is on canonical frames);\n  that every kernel reconstructs canonical frames (two are named; the\n  walker is excluded); that today's stack kernel delivers a tail effect\n  through `K` (it does not; 6c-pre adds it).\n- **Private access.** Every function a test names is public; no `#'`.\n- **Unverified.** 4b stage 2 is not landed: rows 3-4, 7-12, 17, 24 are\n  written against the evaluator design's §1.4-1.6 shapes and the stage-1\n  branch and must be re-read when stage 2 lands. The seam of §4.3 is a\n  specification of a change to `stack.cljc`, not a reading of code that\n  exists. **Amended after 6b (2026-10-13):** 4b stage 2 landed before\n  6b, and rows 3-4, 7-12, 17 and 24 now run on the reference lane (89\n  labelled row × segment executions, 85 distinct builder/wrapper setups\n  since rows 3 and 24 share one builder by design); rows 10-11 were\n  re-read and corrected (§5.2, B1). The 6b harness ran on the JVM only;\n  Node and Dart execution of `rows.cljc`'s transport and the new harness\n  file remain the three-host gate of §8.2 item 9, not established by the\n  reviews.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Findings and proposed amendments\n\nReviewer rulings (codex gpt-6.1-sol, 2026-10-10): F1, F4, U2, U4, U5,\nR1, R2, R7, C1 **ACCEPT**; F2, F3, U1, U3, U6, R3, R4, R5, R6, R8, R9\n**ACCEPT WITH CHANGES**, applied below; dropping `:yin.k/engine`\n**ACCEPTED**; the merged-`E` free environment **REJECTED**, replaced by\n§4.4/§6.3.\n\n### 10.1 To the frozen design (`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md`)\n\n**F1 — §4.2 row names (ACCEPT).** Replace \"`:regs` becomes `[segment-A\npc env-cref window-cref k-cref deliver-D]` for an `act` and a sibling\n`:tail-state [segment-A site k-cref deliver-D]` is added\" with: \"the\nframe rows are `:act [segment-A pc env-cref window-cref k-cref rd]` and\n`:tail [segment-A site k-cref]`, the kind being the delivery\ndiscriminant, so `deliver` is not a slot; `:window` is a keyed row\n`[r₁ V₁ r₂ V₂ …]` sorted by `r` in which a nil-valued register is a\npresent `[:lit nil]`; `:kframe` is `[segment-A pc env-cref window-cref\nrd]`; `:stack` and `stack-base` are removed; the root gains `free-env`,\nthe task's initial free environment (continuation design §2, §6.3).\"\n\n**F2 — §4.2 the validator (ACCEPT WITH CHANGES).** After \"and pending\ncompatibility with `reason`\" insert: \"The validator is the pure function\n`(defect frame code context task)` of `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md`\n§3: `task` is decoded task context (the parked key, the live FFI call\nids, the free environment), never an engine; its rules are ordered,\nstructural checks first, current-window rules on `act` only, return\nwindows separately, and every `E` is checked to agree with the task's\nfree environment off the lexical names; it runs after canonical\nnumbering, as the carrier orders it.\"\n\n**F3 — §4.3 foreign engines (ACCEPT WITH CHANGES).** After\n\"`lift(lower(frame)) = frame` holds for admitted canonical frames, as\ntoday.\" insert: \"The register kernel pairs physical and virtual\nregisters pc by pc from `v` and `R(v)`, recovers every destination from\nA's call (its `dest` is physical), and resolves each return frame's\nimage and relative pc independently; the stack kernel corresponds the\nnon-push instructions of `H(v)` with `v`, slices its operand stack\n`[previous-base, base_i)` per return frame and `[last-base, count)` for\nthe current activation, and needs a tail-completion seam to deliver a\ntail state through `K` (continuation design §4.3, slice 6c-pre); both\nkernels' positional frames become `E` through the owner chain and\n`bind-params`, with the task's free environment carried explicitly. The\nAST walker is not a frame reconstructor.\"\n\n**F4 — §10 phase 6 (ACCEPT).** Replace \"the row carrier amended\" with\n\"the row carrier amended to body version 3 with one profile,\n`{:yin.code/contract \"v4\" :yin.k/version 1}`, carrying canonical vectors\nby A, `:yin.k/trees` and the task's free environment, never a native\nimage (continuation design §2, §6)\"; after \"through their layouts\"\ninsert \"(the register and stack kernels, the latter with its\ntail-completion seam; the walker is excluded)\".\n\n### 10.2 To UCF (`yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`)\n\n**U1 — §7.3.4 `:505-527` (ACCEPT WITH CHANGES).** Replace `:511`\n\"`:yin.k/carried` carries vectors, not batches.\" with \"`:yin.k/carried`\nand the body's code map carry **register-shaped canonical vectors keyed\nby A**, never batches, never datoms projected from a vector, and never a\nnative stack or register image: a receiver of either native kernel\nderives its image from A (derivations design §2-3). `:yin.k/requires\n:yin.k/trees` maps every address in `:yin.k/segments` to the non-empty\nset of tree addresses the emitter's `source-index` held for it\n(derivations design §9.2).\" **Replace `:513-527`** (\"Loading a fetched\nvector has two paths … nothing may depend on which path a conforming\nengine takes.\") with: \"Loading a fetched vector has **one path**: the\ndirect decode of the canonical vector into an image through the one\nvalidator (`yin.vm.semantic-register.code/well-formed?`). The projection\npath (vector → datoms → loader) is retired (owner ruling 2026-09-18;\n`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md` §3.4). The conformance obligation is\nthat the two AST projection lanes and the direct vector load yield one\nimage and address (`load_paths_test.cljc`).\"\n\n**U2 — §7.4.1 (ACCEPT).** At its head: \"Superseded for the\nregister-shaped machine by `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md` §4.1 (two\nstate columns, `act` and `tail`, and the `deliver` record); the table\nbelow describes the stack-shaped machine, which leaves at that design's\nphase 8.\" Replace the `:yin.k/frame` example with §1.2's keyed form.\n\n**U3 — §7.4.2 (ACCEPT WITH CHANGES).** Replace, in the opening\nparagraph, \"absolute stack depth and environment key sets at a pc depend\non the *activation* — a closure enters with the caller's residual\noperand stack and an environment formed from its captured environment\nplus arguments (`apply-call`), and §2.6 enforces no equal stack heights\nat joins, so the same pc is legitimately reached at many depths\" with\n\"the saved window's values, the environment's bindings and the depth of\n`K` at a pc depend on the *activation*; the same pc is legitimately\nreached under many windows and environments\". Replace \"E, St, and every\nK frame's `:stack-base` and env travel in the value\" with \"E, the saved\nwindow, and every K frame's window, `rd` and env travel in the value, as\ndoes the task's free environment\". In the static map replace\n`[sp :yin.safepoint/stack-effect -1]` with `[sp :yin.safepoint/def #{…}]\n[sp :yin.safepoint/use #{…}] [sp :yin.safepoint/live #{…}]`; in the\nrules replace \"`stack-effect` and `lexically-required` are derived from\nthe canonical vector alone, by the same static walk the linearizer\nperforms (§5.3). They are identical for every engine and checkable\nagainst the reference machine by a conformance test that runs the\nsegment to each safepoint and compares the observed deltas and E-reads.\nAbsolute depth, activation bases, captured environments, and physical\nlayouts are not claimed\" with \"`def`, `use`, `live` and\n`lexically-required` are derived from the canonical vector alone\n(`yin.vm.semantic-register.analysis`). They are identical for every\nengine and checkable against the reference machine by a conformance test\nthat runs the segment to each safepoint and compares the saved window's\nkey set with `live − {rd}` and the E-reads. Window values, environments,\n`K` and physical layouts are not claimed\"; replace \"physical slot →\ncanonical slot\" with \"physical location → virtual register\n(continuation design §4)\".\n\n**U4 — §7.4.3 (ACCEPT).** After \"Three rules make these resumable\":\n\"A pending rides on an `act` state (write-result delivery) or a `tail`\nstate (through-return delivery); the variants are unchanged, and\n`:yin.k/registers R` of the version-2 amendment is the `act`/`tail`\nstate of the frozen design §4.2.\"\n\n**U5 — §7.6 (ACCEPT).** In §7.6.1's example add\n`:yin.k/trees {A #{tree-address …}}  ; §7.6.1a` and a new §7.6.1a with\nthe phase-5 §9.2 UCF row text verbatim, including its completeness\nclause (absent or empty is incomplete).\n\n**U6 — §7.11 (ACCEPT WITH CHANGES).** In \"Safepoint conformance\nharness\" replace \"including `lift ∘ lower = id`, the static\n`stack-effect`/`lexically-required` checks\" with \"including `lift ∘\nlower = id` on canonical frames, the static `def`/`use`/`live` and\n`lexically-required` checks, the frame validator on every frame, and\n**cross-engine canonical equality**: one program at one safepoint lifts\nto row sets with equal root ids after the harness's specified\nre-emission (continuation design §5.5) from the semantic-register kernel\nand from the stack and register kernels through their layouts, the stack\nkernel with its tail-completion seam\". In §7.11.1 \"Safepoint\nreconstruction\", replace \"Separately prepare two independently\nimplemented UCF engines\" with \"Separately prepare the three engines\nintended to reconstruct canonical frames (the semantic-register kernel;\nthe register kernel; the stack kernel after its tail-completion seam)\"\nand \"the frame, pending wait, next result, and effect trace must agree\nin both directions\" with \"the canonical row set after re-emission, the\npending wait, next result and effect trace must agree across all three\nand in every lowering direction\".\n\n### 10.3 To the row-carrier draft (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`)\n\nThe inventory below is every clause the grep of the draft for\n\"legacy\", \"version 0/1/2\", \"semantic profile only / semantic-only\",\n\"does not translate\", \"unchanged / do not move\", \"other profile\",\n\"registry\" found operative; each is replaced or deleted.\n\n**R1 — §2 `:50-81` (ACCEPT).** Replace the section with: \"**Body\nversion 3, one profile.** The carrier is body version 3 and reads no\nother version (owner, 2026-10-11: no legacy): a body of version 0, 1 or\n2 is `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` at the gate and is never normalised or\nupgraded. The profile map is `{:yin.code/contract \"v4\" :yin.k/version 1}`;\nthere is no engine key, because the frame is the canonical\nregister-shaped machine's and the executing kernel is the receiving\ncomposition's choice: the stack and register kernels lower the same rows\nthrough their layouts (`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md`\n§2.1, §4). Any other profile map is `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`.\"\n\n**R1a — status paragraph `:18-20`.** Replace \"Body versions 0, 1 and 2\nkeep their grammar, readers and bytes; this document proposes a fourth\nbody version whose carrier is rows\" with \"This document specifies body\nversion 3, the one carrier; older bodies are not read.\"\n\n**R2 — §5.2 `:298-317` (ACCEPT).** Replace with continuation §2.3's\ntable and notes.\n\n**R3 — §5.1 `:287-296` (ACCEPT WITH CHANGES).** Replace the `:reified`\nrow with `| :reified | act-cref | a captured continuation: the decoded\nact state of frozen §4.2, no pending |`; replace \"`{:yin.k/tag\n:yin.k/frame :yin.k/registers R}`\" and \"`{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/frame\n:yin.k/parked-id P}`: resolves in the task's parked table; never\nduplicates `R`\" with the keyed `act` state and \"resolves in the task's\nparked table; never duplicates the state\"; replace \"A reified value's\nregisters are validated in captured-value context (amendment §7): its pc\nmust be a `:current-continuation` site's successor, not a wait or park\nsafepoint\" with \"A reified value's act is validated in context\n`:captured` (continuation §3.2 P3): its `pc−1` is a `:current-continuation`\nand it has no pending\"; and replace \"a reified value becomes a\nreceiver-owned native continuation from `R`\" with \"from its act\".\n\n**R4 — §5.4 `:361` (ACCEPT WITH CHANGES).** Replace `:parked-table |\nP₁ regs-cref₁ P₂ regs-cref₂ …` with `P₁ state-cref₁ …` (an `:act` or a\n`:tail`), and add: \"Validation of a parked state reads decoded task\ncontext, never an engine: the table key `P` and the set of call ids the\ntask's live `:ffi`/`:ffi-request` pendings carry are the `task` input of\n`frame/defect` (continuation §3.1), under which an `:act` at a `:park`\nsite is an explicit park and an `:act` at an `:ffi-call` site or a\n`:tail` at a tail call under a live call id is FFI bookkeeping.\"\n\n**R5 — §5.5 `:383-401` (ACCEPT WITH CHANGES).** Replace the root with\ncontinuation §2.6 (adds `free-env-cref`; `contract` is the one profile\nmap; `code` is `{A \"v4\"-vector}`; `requires` gains `:yin.k/trees`); in\n`:394` replace \"`requires ; plain: UCF §7.6.1's map, unchanged`\" with\n\"`requires ; plain: UCF §7.6.1's map plus :yin.k/trees`\".\n\n**R6 — §8 step 7 `:661-667` (ACCEPT WITH CHANGES).** Replace with:\n\"**Frames.** Every `:act`, `:tail`, `:kframe`, `:reified` and\nparked-table state is decoded to its keyed frame and judged by\n`yin.vm.semantic-register.frame/defect` with its context (`:wait`,\n`:captured`, `:parked`) and the decoded task context (parked key, live\nFFI call ids, the root's free environment); a defect is\n`:yin.k/undecodable` with the rule's kind and the row path (continuation\n§3). This step runs after canonical numbering (step 6) and before\npending validation (step 8).\"\n\n**R6a — §8 step 1 `:618-623`.** Replace \"then every profile map against\nthe registry (only the semantic profile is supported, §2)\" with \"then\nevery profile map against the one profile map of §2\".\n\n**R7 — `:63-68` and §10.4 `:774-777` (ACCEPT).** Delete \"Code stamps\n`\"v3\"`, `\"b2\"`, `\"r2\"` and the inner version 1 do not move: this is a\nchange of carrier, not of instruction, scheduler, or value semantics,\nexactly the distinction the amendment draws between its versions and\nexecutable code.\" Replace §10.4 with: \"**Contract stamp.** The code\ncontract carried is `\"v4\"`, the register-shaped machine's; the carrier\nversion is 3; the two are independent, and neither names a legacy\nvalue.\"\n\n**R8 — §9 `:737-746` \"Legacy correspondence\" and §10.8 `:797-801`\n(ACCEPT WITH CHANGES).** Delete both: there is no legacy reader and no\nnormalisation tool. In §10.3 `:769-773` delete \"Versions 0, 1 and 2\nremain compatibility inputs. One canonical nested projection of version\n3 is kept for single-message channels and must stay exactly mutually\ninverse with the rows.\" and \"Retiring old readers is an owner\nversion-support decision\", leaving \"Rows are the carrier authority.\"\nIn §9's law list delete \"Exact mutual inversion is required only between\nversion 3 and its one canonical nested projection (§10.3)\".\n\n**R9 — §10.6 `:782-788`, §10.7 `:792-796`, §2 `:70-81`, §10's\nproposals list `:821-834`, \"Still open\" `:836-837`, §11 `:839-852`\n(ACCEPT WITH CHANGES).** §10.6, **whole item**: replace its opening\n\"**Foreign-engine value profiles.** Required before any implementation\nbrief claims heterogeneous execution conformance, and not drafted here\"\nwith \"**Foreign-engine value profiles.** Required before an\nimplementation brief claims execution conformance for an engine\n**outside the three repository lanes** (the semantic-register kernel and\nthe stack and register kernels, whose reconstruction is the layouts of\n`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md` §4 and needs no value\nprofile), and not drafted here\"; keep its list of what such a profile\nmust cover; and replace \"version 3, like version 2, does\nnot translate between kernels\" with \"version 3 carries the canonical\nframe; a stack or register kernel reconstructs it through its layout\n(continuation §4), which is heterogeneous reconstruction, not\ntranslation between native formats; foreign-engine *value* profiles for\nengines outside this repository remain a separate document\". §10.7:\ndelete (\"there are no other profile tables\"). Proposals list: delete\n\"semantic-only initial scope\"; add \"one engine-less profile (continuation\n§2.1)\" and \"the explicit root `free-env` (§6.3)\". \"Still open\": replace\nwith \"none beyond the work-bound limits (§10.10)\". §11: replace \"Code\nidentity, the contract stamp and profile registry, safepoints,\" with\n\"Code identity (A), safepoints,\"; delete \"versions 0-2\" references.\n\n**R10 — §5.5 `:421-426` custody paragraph.** Replace \"a version-3 fork\nroot is admitted exactly as a version-0 body is, proposes nothing, and\nis never upgraded to custody\" with \"a fork root proposes nothing and is\nnever upgraded to custody; an exclusive-only receiver refuses it as\n`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`\".\n\n**R11 — §3 `:115-134`.** Keep verbatim, including \"**body version 3\nuses BLAKE3 permanently**\" and the per-id algorithm check (the reviewer\nasked that this explicit rule survive).\n\n**U7 — scope and precedence of UCF's historical legacy-body clauses\n(new).** UCF keeps clauses that describe bodies the row carrier never\nreads: §7.2.1 (versions 0 and 1, `:166-321`), the version-0/1 custody\ngate and fork rules (`:234-290`), §7.4.3 r5's version-0 notes (`:966-970`:\n\"A version-0 entry without a phase reads as `:running`\", \"The landed\nversion-0 reader breaches both\"), §7.7.4's `nil`-header version-0 lift\nand recovery of legacy fork bytes (`:1781`, `:1806-1807`, `:1844`), and\n§7.11.1's version-0/1 interoperation rows (`:3051-3057`, `:3095-3096`).\nInsert at the head of §7.2.1: \"**Scope (2026-10-11).** Body versions 0,\n1 and 2 and every clause of this document that admits, reads, recovers\nor interoperates with them are **historical**: they describe the\nstack-shaped machine's handoff, which leaves at\n`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md` §10 phase 8. The row carrier of body\nversion 3 (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md` as amended by\n`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md`) reads no other version and\ntakes **precedence** over any sentence below that implies otherwise; a\nconflict is resolved in its favour without further amendment. **Not\nhistorical**: the custody, fencing and operation-id semantics (§7.7.8,\n§7.9, §7.4.3's operation ids) travel in version 3 as the root's\n`custody` slot (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md` §5.5) and stay\noperative; where those sections say 'a version-1 body' read, for version\n3, 'a root whose custody policy is `:yin.k/exclusive`', and where they\nsay 'a version-0 body' or 'fork', 'a root whose policy is `:yin.k/fork`'\n(§5.5's table). The code stamp `{:yin.code/contract \"v3\" :yin.k/version\n0}` wherever this document shows it (§7.2's example `:121`, §7.2.1\n`:183`, §7.3.3 `:232-236`) is the stack-shaped machine's; the\nregister-shaped machine's contract is `\"v4\"`\n(`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md` §6), the one the version-3 profile map\nnames, and §7.2's envelope example is superseded for version 3 by the\nrow carrier's wire unit (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md` §3, ids under\nJing's default digest).\" The same grep (`legacy`, `version-0`,\n`version 0`, `versions 0`, `version 1`, `recovery`) found, besides the\nclauses above: the export-recovery object's version-1 format\n(`:1789-1807`), which is phase 7's to re-base on the row carrier and is\nnamed in §8.4 as an entry condition, not amended here; publication\nfailure recovery (`:1741`), completion recovery (`:2417`) and the\nledger's \"legacy destructive readers\" (`:2774-2775`), which concern the\ndriver, the ledger protocol and the ledger reader, not a body version,\nand are untouched; and the status paragraph `:39-42`, covered by the\nscope statement's \"every clause\".\n\n### 10.4 To code-as-tuples (`yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`) §7.2\n\n**C1 (ACCEPT).** Replace the first paragraph with: \"The vector-to-datoms\nprojection path is **retired** (owner ruling 2026-09-18; frozen §3.4\n'One vector validator'). There is one load path for a vector, the direct\none, and two AST projection lanes (datom lane, row lane) that produce\nthe vector; all three feed one validator\n(`yin.vm.semantic-register.code/well-formed?`). UCF §7.3.4's projection\npath is superseded accordingly.\" Replace obligation 2's second sentence\nwith \"loading the vector produced by either AST lane and the vector\nloaded directly yield the same image and address (`load_paths_test.cljc`)\",\nand obligation 3's \"both paths refuse with the same defect\" with \"the\ndirect vector load refuses with the published defect (rule and pc) for\nevery rule of the frozen §3.4\". Obligations 1 and 4 stand.\n\n### 10.5 Checked and consistent (no amendment)\n\nFrozen §2.4 delivery shapes against the 4b branch; frozen §4.1 two state\ncolumns; the evaluator design §1.3-1.6; the draft's §3, §4, §5.1 (except\n`:reified`), §5.3, §6, §7 and §8 steps 1-6, 8-12; v2 amendment §2's `E`\nshape; the phase-5 §9.2 `:yin.k/trees` text; the register kernel's\n`:return-result` delivery (`register.cljc:458-468`).\n\n### 10.6 Amendments after slice 6b (revision 4, 2026-10-13)\n\nEach item names the clause, what changed, and why; the clause itself\ncarries the same marker. Sources are the engineer report's design bugs\n(B1-B4) and deviations (1-10) and the three codex reviews' rulings.\n\n| # | Clause | What changed | Why |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| B1 | §5.2 rows 10-12, the install paragraph; §8.2 item 3 | row 10 is child `:blocked`, phase `:parked`; row 11 is the `:running` wire phase set by hand on row 10's machine; the lower's install contract (phase set, response and image, child `:blocked`) is stated | `advance-install` retains a blocked child at `:parked` and links a halted or parked one in the same sweep; the promised states were unreachable |\n| B2 | §5.2 wrapper paragraph, row 21 | the mechanical wrapper rule has one stated exception: row 21 under `:depth-3` answers `[3 [2 [1 1]]]` | `nest`'s parameter is `x` and shadows the free `x` before `f` is created; the reference run itself answers 1 |\n| B3 | §5.2 refusal rows; §8.2 item 7 | the `:tail` fourth slot is `:row-shape` at structure step 2, and the tamper is a raw row edit with rehash | the row table fixes `:tail` at three slots before S1 runs; `emit` drops extra abstract slots silently |\n| B4 | §7.1 | the lift's order is quiescence, kind, free environment, walk, code, dependency closure, trees, task, frames, emit | segments and contexts are known only after the walk |\n| D1 | §6.1, §8.2 depends-on | `:source-index` is a lift input from the composition | the kernel holds no source index |\n| D2 | §7.1 | refusals are thrown `ex-info` | consistency with `decode`/`emit` |\n| D3 | §2.5, §2.6, §7.2 | custody nil at the root; non-nil is `:yin.k/unsupported :custody` at step 10 | exclusive/holder is phase 7 |\n| D4 | §2.5, §5.2 row 17 | linked registry entries do not travel; the receiver's registry is a composition prerequisite and is kept as given | no registry slot in the row table |\n| D5 | §7.1, §7.2 | discharge is per context over live code (`live-pcs`), answered by `E`, the one active store, primitive, installing export, registry; lift only | codex F3 (union over stores was unsound) and F6 (whole-body scan refused valid states) |\n| D6 | §2.5 | the store slice is the whole root store; every parked record travels; module stores by reachability | conservative and what the code does |\n| D7 | §6.3 | the retention seam landed; the lift reads the kernel's `free-env` field by default, the option overrides, E2 catches a disagreeing override | 4b stage 3 landed before 6b |\n| D8 | §7.2 | the lower's four stages, each across the whole tree before the next, all before attachment; the refusal-kind table including `:yin.k/stores` | codex F1, F2, F4, F7, F8: every carrier defect must be a named refusal before any attach |\n| D9 | §5.2 row 18 | the fixture's module store is symbol-keyed and hand-built | codex asked for the synthetic label |\n| D10 | §9 | 4b stage 2 landed; rows 3-4, 7-12, 17, 24 run; three-host gate still open | honesty |\n\n**Note on `handoff.cljc` (no edit).** The engineer report said\n`export-task` builds installing names as `(symbol (str m \"/\" x))`, a\nnil-namespace symbol that never equals `m/x`. Read at\n`handoff.cljc:1480-1488`, the code is `(symbol (str (name m) \"/\" (name\nx)))`. The one-argument `symbol` of Clojure, ClojureScript and the\npinned ClojureDart core splits its string at `/` into namespace and\nname, so this yields the qualified `host.mod/f` with namespace\n`\"host.mod\"`, equal to `'host.mod/f`; codex round 1 evaluated it on the\nJVM and found the same. The claim is withdrawn; the old form is not a\nbug. `rows.cljc`'s `installing-names` uses the two-argument `(symbol\n(name mn) (name x))`, which is equivalent. `handoff.cljc` is not edited\nby this phase (§8.5).\n\n---\n\n## 11. Owner questions\n\nRevisions 2-3 left none: the reviewer accepted the engine-less profile\nand rejected the merged-`E` free environment. **Revision 4 (after 6b)\nlists the design choices the merged code made that no ruling covers.**\nEach is stated as the code behaves today; none is decided by this\ndocument.\n\n- **Q1, install children, the parked-child scenario.** The engine never\n  retains an install child at a quiescent safepoint except `:blocked` at\n  phase `:parked` (§5.2 B1). The carrier still admits phase `:running`\n  (never engine-reachable at a safepoint) and the lower refuses any\n  child kind but `:blocked`. Should the row table and both checks\n  narrow to the one reachable state (phase `:parked`, child `:blocked`),\n  or does the owner want a reachable parked-child install scenario,\n  which is an engine change (`advance-install`), with row 11 rewritten\n  to it?\n- **Q2, census agreement on lower.** The draft §8 step 9 requires the\n  carried `requires`/`code` to agree **exactly** with a census recomputed\n  from the rows (a declared segment not reached, or a reached segment\n  not declared, is `:yin.k/undecodable`). `check-dependencies!` checks\n  only that `:yin.k/segments` equals the key set of `code`; a vector\n  carried but referenced by no row is accepted and attached. Accept the\n  narrower check, or require the recomputed census?\n- **Q3, install response verification.** The draft §5.4 asks the\n  receiver to verify that the install's link id equals `parent`, that\n  the image hashes to the address it is delivered under and is the image\n  the manifest names, and that format, contract and the child's profile\n  agree. `check-install!` verifies status, manifest name, export shape\n  and that the image loads; not the link id, not the image's address\n  against the manifest. Accept the narrower contract for phase 6, or\n  extend it before phase 7 (which re-bases the export-recovery object)?\n- **Q4, operation ids and correlation on lower.** The draft §8 step 8's\n  op-id rules (below `next-op-seq`, unique across the tree, absent\n  without origin) and its correlation agreement (cursor-cell stream,\n  envelope ids, module names) are not implemented; op-ids travel\n  verbatim, and pending compatibility is only P2's reason check. With\n  fork-only custody there is no `next-op-seq`; is this phase 7's with\n  the custody arm, or a 6-era gap to close?\n- **Q5, no re-discharge on lower.** Free names are discharged at lift\n  only (§7.1); the lower checks carried segments, trees, store census,\n  closures and primitives, and a name a closure resolves through the\n  registry is a composition prerequisite (§2.5). A receiver lacking that\n  registry entry accepts the carrier and fails at run time\n  (`Unable to resolve symbol`). Accept, or require the lower to\n  re-discharge registry-answered names against the receiver's registry\n  before attachment?\n- **Q6, conservatism of the live-code rule.** `live-pcs` follows both\n  successor edges of every branch and enters every reachable closure\n  body, with no constant propagation. The harness's `(if false (fn []\n  ghost) (next-of c))` case transports only because the lift happens at\n  a safepoint **after** the branch (the resume pc is past it, so the\n  dead arm is unreachable by edges); the same program lifted at a\n  safepoint **before** the branch would refuse `ghost` although the arm\n  can never run. Is \"reachable by control-flow edges, never by value\"\n  the intended precision, accepting the false refusals it implies for\n  constant-dead code?\n- **Q7, host-class primitives behind a shadow.** `:host-state-primitive`\n  refuses only when the name reaches the primitive step of the answering\n  order; the same name bound in `E` or the active store lifts (codex\n  round 2 confirmed). Intended, or should a host-class primitive name\n  refuse regardless of shadowing?\n- **Q8, preflight attachment cost.** `attaches?` validates and analyses\n  every carried vector (and every install image) by attaching it into a\n  throwaway receiver, and restore attaches it again. Codex found it pure\n  and only duplicated CPU. Accept, or ask the kernel for a validate-only\n  entry point?\n- **Q9, Law 2 outside a fresh receiver.** The id counter is adopted as\n  `max(local, carried)`; a receiver whose construction mints more ids\n  than the carrier relifts to a different root (draft §9 already scopes\n  the law to the fresh-receiver context). No harness case has a\n  non-fresh receiver. Add one as a documented non-law, or leave it?\n- **Q10, the `:running` wire phase's future.** If Q1 narrows the phase\n  set, row 11 disappears from the matrix and the `:install` row's phase\n  slot becomes a constant; if not, row 11 stays a synthetic case. Which?\n\n---\n\n## 12. Portability notes for the implementer\n\n1. **Nil-valued window keys (Dart, Node).** The window is built with\n   `select-keys` and encoded as `[r [:lit nil]]`; never filter `some?`\n   values; `frame-test/nil-valued-register-is-a-member` over row 22\n   (`(pair nil (next-of c))`, the nil operand evaluated before the read)\n   is the canary. Membership is always by `contains?`/key sets, never\n   `(get W r)`.\n2. **`for` over large seqs (Dart).** Row sets of the C4 modules run to\n   thousands of rows; build with `mapv`/`into`/`reduce`.\n3. **Reader whitespace before closers (Dart).** Pinned row sets are\n   Clojure data in `.cljc`, never EDN strings.\n4. **Duplicate protocol params (Dart).** `gc-children [_ _x]`\n   (`srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:386`); any new protocol method\n   in `layout` likewise.\n5. **Multi-key `assoc` on nil (Dart).** Frames and windows start from\n   `{}` (`handoff.cljc:1455-1457` records the trap).\n6. **Reader conditionals.** `#?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default)`\n   with `:cljd` first; `:clj`-only source-reading tests as `#?(:cljd nil :clj …)`.\n7. **Private var access (Dart).** Every function named in §8 is public;\n   no `#'`.\n8. **Sorted keys.** Window and bindings rows are sorted by Jing\n   canonical bytes of the key, never by host `sort` on mixed types.\n9. **Numeric classes (Node).** `pc`, `rd`, `r`, `site` are exact\n   integers; compare with `cbor/numeric-kind` before `=` on wire values\n   (`ucf/profile-engine`, `ucf.cljc:77-91`, is the template).\n10. **Fresh worktree.** `mise trust` and `npm ci` before the Node lane.\n","numLines":509,"startLine":1610,"totalLines":2118}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_014FitVYLe5mCRTPDkL4NtyZ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011Cfz2NFJX3wi6uu9yhji4B","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Cd5CfRce4sHJLMpUmYrFdt","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7plan/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":632,"cache_read_input_tokens":34778,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":632},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f7716a8d-bcb9-4b49-9ad0-ed6a5daed388","uuid":"435a77c5-ef88-4920-ad0d-d073a4342e41","timestamp":"2026-10-13T09:47:18.772Z","request_id":"req_011Cfz2NEzRPWZ4Lm4LA2CVt","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01Cd5CfRce4sHJLMpUmYrFdt":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7plan/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Cd5CfRce4sHJLMpUmYrFdt","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t(ns yin.vm.semantic-register.layout\n2\t  \"Phase 6c (docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md §4):\n3\t   the de Bruijn register and stack kernels reconstruct the canonical\n4\t   frame of the register machine through their layouts, and lower it\n5\t   back.\n6\t\n7\t   Static half, per segment A: `segment` derives R(A) and H(A) and reads\n8\t   off the register pairing (`phys`, the restricted inverse `virt`, the\n9\t   kernel's `live` as live-out with tail boundaries `[]`), the stack\n10\t   correspondence (`h->a`, `a->h`, every `:push` removed) and the operand\n11\t   layout `stack-layout` (the values pushed and not yet consumed while\n12\t   control is inside a pc's expression). A `space` indexes segments by\n13\t   the kernels' image identities, R and H, which are checksums: A is the\n14\t   only code identity.\n15\t\n16\t   Dynamic half: `lift-state` reads a kernel's wait entry, parked record\n17\t   or reified payload as the canonical `act` or `tail` payload the\n18\t   semantic-register evaluator mints (`{:segment A :pc :env :window :k\n19\t   :deliver}`, `{:segment A :site :k :deliver}`, engine fields carried);\n20\t   `lower-state` is its inverse. The register kernel's return\n21\t   destinations are recovered from A, never from the physical `:dest`;\n22\t   the stack kernel's operand stack is sliced `[previous-base, base)` per\n23\t   frame and lowered with cumulative bases. `E` is the free environment\n24\t   merged under the chain's positional frames (§4.4); the inverse reads\n25\t   each frame back from `E` by the chain, never the free environment.\n26\t\n27\t   Task level: `canonical-task` presents a quiescent kernel as a\n28\t   semantic-register machine for `yin.vm.ucf.rows/lift-task`;\n29\t   `kernel-task` restores a machine `rows/lower-task` produced into a\n30\t   fresh kernel; `lift-task` and `lower-task` compose them with the row\n31\t   carrier, the lower refusing what a kernel cannot hold before any\n32\t   stream is attached. A layout never runs code, never fills a binding\n33\t   from the receiver and never invents a safepoint (§4.5).\"\n34\t  (:require [dao.jing :as jing]\n35\t            [yin.vm.debruijn-code :as dcode]\n36\t            [yin.vm.debruijn-register-code :as rcode]\n37\t            [yin.vm.debruijn.register :as register]\n38\t            [yin.vm.debruijn.stack :as stack]\n39\t            [yin.vm :as vm]\n40\t            [yin.vm.engine :as engine]\n41\t            [yin.vm.module :as module]\n42\t            [yin.vm.semantic-register.analysis :as analysis]\n43\t            [yin.vm.semantic-register.code :as code]\n44\t            [yin.vm.semantic-register.derive :as derive]\n45\t            [yin.vm.ucf.rows :as rows]\n46\t            [yin.vm.values :as values]))\n47\t\n48\t\n49\t(defn- refuse!\n50\t  [status kind data]\n51\t  (throw (ex-info \"Layout refused\" (merge data (assoc {} :yin.k/status status :yin.k/kind kind)))))\n52\t\n53\t\n54\t;; =============================================================================\n55\t;; Static half: one segment\n56\t;; =============================================================================\n57\t\n58\t(defn- operands\n59\t  \"The register operands of an A tuple, destination first, then its uses\n60\t   in table order.\"\n61\t  [t]\n62\t  (into (if-some [r (code/rd t)] [r] []) (code/uses t)))\n63\t\n64\t\n65\t(defn- register-operands\n66\t  \"The `:reg`/`:regs` operands of an R tuple in table order (the `:live`\n67\t   slot is `:data`, never paired).\"\n68\t  [t]\n69\t  (into []\n70\t        (mapcat (fn [[[_ kind] x]] (case kind :reg [x] :regs x [])))\n71\t        (map vector (get rcode/opcode-table (nth t 0)) (rest t))))\n72\t\n73\t\n74\t(defn register-pairs\n75\t  \"`[pc virtual physical]` for every register operand of every pc: A's\n76\t   tuple and R's name their registers in the same slots (phase-5 §2.4).\"\n77\t  [v image]\n78\t  (into []\n79\t        (mapcat (fn [pc]\n80\t                  (map (fn [x s] [pc x s])\n81\t                       (operands (nth v pc))\n82\t                       (register-operands (nth (:instructions image) pc)))))\n83\t        (range (count v))))\n84\t\n85\t\n86\t(defn- phys-maps\n87\t  \"Per body, `{virtual physical}` read off the pairs; the first pc that\n88\t   names a virtual wins (register-pairing checks they agree).\"\n89\t  [bodies pairs]\n90\t  (reduce (fn [acc [pc x s]]\n91\t            (let [b (code/body-index bodies pc)]\n92\t              (if (contains? (nth acc b) x) acc (assoc-in acc [b x] s))))\n93\t          (vec (repeat (count bodies) {}))\n94\t          pairs))\n95\t\n96\t\n97\t(defn correspondence\n98\t  \"§4.3 static item 1: H with every `:push` removed corresponds one to one,\n99\t   in order, with A. Answers `{:a->h [h-pc …] :h->a {h-pc a-pc}}`.\"\n100\t  [h]\n101\t  (reduce (fn [acc hpc]\n102\t            (if (= :push (nth (nth h hpc) 0))\n103\t              acc\n104\t              (-> acc\n105\t                  (update :a->h conj hpc)\n106\t                  (assoc-in [:h->a hpc] (count (:a->h acc))))))\n107\t          {:a->h [] :h->a {}}\n108\t          (range (count h))))\n109\t\n110\t\n111\t(def ^:private pushing-ops\n112\t  \"The parse nodes whose children before the current one are pushed:\n113\t   `:call` (the operator and earlier operands), `:ffi-call` (earlier\n114\t   operands) and `:stream-put` (the target, while the value runs).\"\n115\t  #{:call :ffi-call :stream-put})\n116\t\n117\t\n118\t(defn- stack-layouts\n119\t  \"§4.3 static item 2 for every pc of every body: `{pc [virtual …]}`,\n120\t   bottom to top, over the parse tree's ancestors outermost first.\"\n121\t  [v bodies]\n122\t  (letfn [(walk\n123\t            [out node pending]\n124\t            (let [out (assoc out (:pc node) pending)\n125\t                  pushes? (contains? pushing-ops (:op node))\n126\t                  uses (when pushes? (code/uses (nth v (:pc node))))]\n127\t              (reduce (fn [out [i child]]\n128\t                        (walk out child (if pushes? (into pending (take i uses)) pending)))\n129\t                      out\n130\t                      (map-indexed vector (:children node)))))]\n131\t    (reduce (fn [out body] (walk out (:expr body) [])) {} bodies)))\n132\t\n133\t\n134\t(defn segment\n135\t  \"The static facts of one admitted A vector `v`, for both kernels.\"\n136\t  [v]\n137\t  (let [bodies (code/parse v)\n138\t        r (derive/register-image v)\n139\t        h (:image (derive/stack-image v))\n140\t        pairs (register-pairs v r)]\n141\t    (merge {:vector v\n142\t            :address (jing/segment-key v)\n143\t            :bodies bodies\n144\t            :analysis (analysis/analyze v)\n145\t            :register r\n146\t            :register-id (rcode/register-hash r)\n147\t            :stack h\n148\t            :stack-id (dcode/image-hash h)\n149\t            :pairs pairs\n150\t            :phys (phys-maps bodies pairs)\n151\t            :layouts (stack-layouts v bodies)}\n152\t           (correspondence h))))\n153\t\n154\t\n155\t(defn space\n156\t  \"Segments by A, and A by each kernel's image identity. `segments` is a\n157\t   seq of `segment` results or of A vectors.\"\n158\t  [segments]\n159\t  (reduce (fn [acc s]\n160\t            (let [s (if (map? s) s (segment s))\n161\t                  a (:address s)]\n162\t              (-> acc\n163\t                  (assoc-in [:segments a] s)\n164\t                  (update-in [:register (:register-id s)] (fnil conj #{}) a)\n165\t                  (update-in [:stack (:stack-id s)] (fnil conj #{}) a))))\n166\t          {:segments {} :register {} :stack {}}\n167\t          segments))\n168\t\n169\t\n170\t(defn segment-of\n171\t  \"The segment A names in `space`.\"\n172\t  [space a]\n173\t  (or (get-in space [:segments a])\n174\t      (refuse! :yin.k/unsatisfied :segment {:yin.k/segment a})))\n175\t\n176\t\n177\t(defn- image-segment\n178\t  \"The segment whose `kind` image is `ident`: an identity is a checksum,\n179\t   so two A deriving one image are ambiguous and refused.\"\n180\t  [space kind ident]\n181\t  (let [as (get-in space [kind ident])]\n182\t    (cond\n183\t      (empty? as) (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :unknown-image {:yin.k/image ident})\n184\t      (< 1 (count as)) (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :ambiguous-image {:yin.k/image ident})\n185\t      :else (segment-of space (first as)))))\n186\t\n187\t\n188\t(defn- inst\n189\t  [seg pc]\n190\t  (nth (:vector seg) pc))\n191\t\n192\t\n193\t(defn- body-of\n194\t  [seg pc]\n195\t  (code/body-index (:bodies seg) pc))\n196\t\n197\t\n198\t(defn phys\n199\t  \"`phys(body(pc), x)`: the physical register R names for virtual `x` in\n200\t   the body holding `pc`.\"\n201\t  [seg pc x]\n202\t  (let [s (get (nth (:phys seg) (body-of seg pc)) x)]\n203\t    (when (nil? s) (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :register-layout {:yin.k/pc pc :yin.k/register x}))\n204\t    s))\n205\t\n206\t\n207\t(defn live-out\n208\t  \"`live-out*(p)`: the union of `L(q)` over p's actual successors (empty\n209\t   for a tail call).\"\n210\t  [seg p]\n211\t  (reduce (fn [acc q] (into acc (analysis/live (:analysis seg) q)))\n212\t          #{}\n213\t          (analysis/successors (:vector seg) p)))\n214\t\n215\t\n216\t(defn register-live\n217\t  \"§5.6 item 3: the `live` operand R must carry at boundary `p`,\n218\t   `(sort (map phys (disj live-out*(p) rd)))`, the destination removed as\n219\t   a virtual id before mapping; `[]` at a tail call.\"\n220\t  [seg p]\n221\t  (vec (sort (map #(phys seg p %) (disj (live-out seg p) (code/rd (inst seg p)))))))\n222\t\n223\t\n224\t(defn virt\n225\t  \"The restricted inverse of `phys` at boundary `p`: the virtual of\n226\t   `live-out*(p)` held in physical `s`. A slot outside that image (only a\n227\t   tampered entry names one) reads as the one virtual of the body R maps\n228\t   there, so the frame validator judges the window; an ambiguous slot is\n229\t   refused.\"\n230\t  [seg p s]\n231\t  (let [m (nth (:phys seg) (body-of seg p))\n232\t        live (filterv #(= s (get m %)) (sort (live-out seg p)))\n233\t        named (filterv #(= s (get m %)) (sort (keys m)))]\n234\t    (cond\n235\t      (= 1 (count live)) (first live)\n236\t      (and (empty? live) (= 1 (count named))) (first named)\n237\t      :else (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :register-layout {:yin.k/pc p :yin.k/register s}))))\n238\t\n239\t\n240\t(defn stack-layout\n241\t  \"§4.3: the virtual registers pushed and not yet consumed while control\n242\t   is inside the expression at A pc `p`, bottom to top.\"\n243\t  [seg p]\n244\t  (get (:layouts seg) p))\n245\t\n246\t\n247\t(defn chain\n248\t  \"The closure pcs owning the body that holds `pc`, outermost first.\"\n249\t  [seg pc]\n250\t  (loop [b (body-of seg pc) out ()]\n251\t    (if-let [owner (:owner (nth (:bodies seg) b))]\n252\t      (recur (body-of seg owner) (conj out owner))\n253\t      (vec out))))\n254\t\n255\t\n256\t(defn- params\n257\t  [seg c]\n258\t  (nth (inst seg c) 2))\n259\t\n260\t\n261\t(defn env-of\n262\t  \"§4.4 forward: `E` at `pc` from the positional frames `F` (outermost\n263\t   first, values already canonical), the task's free environment and the\n264\t   activation's module store.\"\n265\t  [seg pc free-env frames store-of]\n266\t  (let [cs (chain seg pc)]\n267\t    (when-not (= (count cs) (count frames))\n268\t      (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :frame-depth {:yin.k/pc pc :yin.k/depth (count frames)}))\n269\t    (cond-> (reduce (fn [e [c f]] (merge e (engine/bind-params (params seg c) f)))\n270\t                    (or free-env {})\n271\t                    (map vector cs frames))\n272\t      (some? store-of) (assoc engine/store-of-key store-of))))\n273\t\n274\t\n275\t(defn frames-of\n276\t  \"§4.4 inverse: `F[i] = (mapv #(get E %) params(cᵢ))` over the chain at\n277\t   `pc`. An outer value a shadowing inner binder hides reads as the inner\n278\t   one; the canonical frame never held it.\"\n279\t  [seg pc env]\n280\t  (mapv (fn [c] (mapv #(get env %) (params seg c))) (chain seg pc)))\n281\t\n282\t\n283\t;; =============================================================================\n284\t;; Kernel dispatch\n285\t;; =============================================================================\n286\t\n287\t(defn kernel-kind\n288\t  \"`:stack` or `:register`, by the kernel's link format.\"\n289\t  [k]\n290\t  (let [f (when (satisfies? module/IModuleKernel k) (:format (module/link-format k)))]\n291\t    (cond\n292\t      (= stack/format-tag f) :stack\n293\t      (= register/format-tag f) :register\n294\t      :else (refuse! :yin.k/unsupported :kernel {:yin.k/format f}))))\n295\t\n296\t\n297\t(defn- image-pc\n298\t  \"`[segment rel-pc]` of absolute kernel pc `pc`.\"\n299\t  [kind k space pc]\n300\t  (let [[ident rel] (or (if (= :stack kind) (stack/image-pc k pc) (register/image-pc k pc))\n301\t                        (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :unknown-image {:yin.k/pc pc}))]\n302\t    [(image-segment space kind ident) rel]))\n303\t\n304\t\n305\t(defn offset\n306\t  \"The offset of segment `seg`'s image in kernel `k`'s code space.\"\n307\t  [kind k seg]\n308\t  (let [ident (if (= :stack kind) (:stack-id seg) (:register-id seg))]\n309\t    (or (if (= :stack kind) (stack/absolute-pc k [ident 0]) (register/absolute-pc k [ident 0]))\n310\t        (refuse! :yin.k/unsatisfied :image-not-attached {:yin.k/segment (:address seg)}))))\n311\t\n312\t\n313\t(def ^:private parking-ops\n314\t  \"A1: the instructions a resume pc may follow, besides a non-tail call.\"\n315\t  #{:stream-next :stream-put :ffi-call :park :current-continuation})\n316\t\n317\t\n318\t(defn- safepoint!\n319\t  \"Refuse `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` unless the A instruction before `pc`\n320\t   parks (a tail call for a tail state): a layout never invents a\n321\t   safepoint (§4.5).\"\n322\t  [seg pc tail?]\n323\t  (let [t (when (and (integer? pc) (< 0 pc) (<= pc (count (:vector seg)))) (inst seg (dec pc)))\n324\t        op (when t (nth t 0))]\n325\t    (when-not (and t\n326\t                   (= (body-of seg (dec pc)) (body-of seg (min pc (dec (count (:vector seg))))))\n327\t                   (if tail?\n328\t                     (code/tail-call? t)\n329\t                     (or (contains? parking-ops op) (and (= :call op) (not (code/tail-call? t))))))\n330\t      (refuse! :yin.k/not-at-safepoint nil {:yin.k/pc pc}))))\n331\t\n332\t\n333\t(defn- h->a\n334\t  \"The A pc of H pc `hpc`; a `:push` has none and is no safepoint.\"\n335\t  [seg hpc]\n336\t  (or (get (:h->a seg) hpc) (refuse! :yin.k/not-at-safepoint nil {:yin.k/pc hpc})))\n337\t\n338\t\n339\t(defn- a->h\n340\t  [seg pc]\n341\t  (nth (:a->h seg) pc))\n342\t\n343\t\n344\t;; =============================================================================\n345\t;; Values: positional closures and kernel continuations, both ways\n346\t;; =============================================================================\n347\t;;\n348\t;; A lift context `cx` is {:kind :kernel :space :free-env :owner}; a lower\n349\t;; context `lx` adds :address, naming a canonical payload's or closure's\n350\t;; :segment as A.\n351\t\n352\t(declare lift-state lower-state canonical-value positional-value)\n353\t\n354\t\n355\t(defn- walk-value\n356\t  \"`x` with its guest collections rebuilt around `f`, which converts\n357\t   closures and continuations.\"\n358\t  [f x]\n359\t  (cond\n360\t    (values/host-typed? x) (f x)\n361\t    (map? x) (into {} (map (fn [[k v]] [(walk-value f k) (walk-value f v)])) x)\n362\t    (vector? x) (mapv #(walk-value f %) x)\n363\t    (set? x) (into #{} (map #(walk-value f %)) x)\n364\t    (seq? x) (apply list (map #(walk-value f %) x))\n365\t    :else x))\n366\t\n367\t\n368\t(defn- canonical-closure\n369\t  \"§4.4: a positional closure as the canonical `{:type :closure :params\n370\t   :entry :segment :env}`: `params` from A's `:closure` at the owner of\n371\t   the entry body, `env` by the forward rule over the closure's frames.\"\n372\t  [cx c]\n373\t  (let [[seg rel] (image-pc (:kind cx) (:kernel cx) (:space cx) (:body-pc c))\n374\t        entry (if (= :stack (:kind cx)) (h->a seg rel) rel)\n375\t        owner (:owner (nth (:bodies seg) (body-of seg entry)))\n376\t        _ (when-not (and owner (= entry (nth (inst seg owner) 3)))\n377\t            (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :closure-origin {:yin.k/entry entry}))\n378\t        ps (params seg owner)]\n379\t    (when-not (= (count ps) (:arity c))\n380\t      (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :closure-binders {:yin.k/entry entry}))\n381\t    {:type :closure :params ps :entry entry :segment (:address seg)\n382\t     :env (env-of seg owner (:free-env cx)\n383\t                  (mapv (fn [f] (mapv #(canonical-value cx %) f)) (:frames c))\n384\t                  (:store-of c))}))\n385\t\n386\t\n387\t(defn- canonical-value*\n388\t  [cx x]\n389\t  (cond\n390\t    (values/closure? x) (values/closure (:owner cx) (canonical-closure cx (values/payload x)))\n391\t    (values/continuation? x)\n392\t    (values/continuation (:owner cx) (assoc (lift-state cx (values/payload x)) :type :reified-continuation))\n393\t    :else x))\n394\t\n395\t\n396\t(defn canonical-value\n397\t  \"Kernel value `x` with every positional closure and kernel continuation\n398\t   reconstructed canonically (cx: a lift context).\"\n399\t  [cx x]\n400\t  (walk-value #(canonical-value* cx %) x))\n401\t\n402\t\n403\t(defn- address-of\n404\t  [lx seg-id]\n405\t  (let [a ((:address lx) seg-id)]\n406\t    (when (nil? a) (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :unaddressed-segment {:yin.k/hint seg-id}))\n407\t    a))\n408\t\n409\t\n410\t(defn- positional-closure\n411\t  \"The inverse of canonical-closure: the entry's absolute pc in the\n412\t   receiving kernel, the frames read back from `env` by the owner's chain.\"\n413\t  [lx c]\n414\t  (let [seg (segment-of (:space lx) (address-of lx (:segment c)))\n415\t        entry (:entry c)\n416\t        owner (:owner (nth (:bodies seg) (body-of seg entry)))\n417\t        store-of (engine/env-store-of (:env c))]\n418\t    (when-not (and owner (= entry (nth (inst seg owner) 3)) (= (vec (:params c)) (vec (params seg owner))))\n419\t      (refuse! :yin.k/undecodable :closure-binders {:yin.k/entry entry}))\n420\t    (cond-> {:type :closure :arity (count (:params c))\n421\t             :body-pc (+ (offset (:kind lx) (:kernel lx) seg) (if (= :stack (:kind lx)) (a->h seg entry) entry))\n422\t             :frames (mapv (fn [f] (mapv #(positional-value lx %) f)) (frames-of seg owner (:env c)))}\n423\t      (some? store-of) (assoc :store-of store-of))))\n424\t\n425\t\n426\t(defn- positional-value*\n427\t  [lx x]\n428\t  (cond\n429\t    (values/closure? x) (values/closure (:owner lx) (positional-closure lx (values/payload x)))\n430\t    (values/continuation? x)\n431\t    (values/continuation (:owner lx) (merge {:type :reified-continuation} (lower-state lx (values/payload x))))\n432\t    :else x))\n433\t\n434\t\n435\t(defn positional-value\n436\t  \"Canonical value `x` with every closure and continuation lowered into\n437\t   the receiving kernel (lx: a lower context).\"\n438\t  [lx x]\n439\t  (walk-value #(positional-value* lx %) x))\n440\t\n441\t\n442\t;; =============================================================================\n443\t;; Lift: a kernel payload as the canonical act or tail\n444\t;; =============================================================================\n445\t\n446\t(def ^:private kernel-keys\n447\t  \"The register payload keys of either kernel; every other key of an\n448\t   entry is the engine's and travels in the pending.\"\n449\t  #{:segment :site-pc :pc :frames :regs :live :continuation :dest :resume-mode\n450\t    :format :hash :image :images :store-of :stack})\n451\t\n452\t\n453\t(defn- canonical-env\n454\t  [cx seg pc frames store-of]\n455\t  (env-of seg pc (:free-env cx) (mapv (fn [f] (mapv #(canonical-value cx %) f)) frames) store-of))\n456\t\n457\t\n458\t(defn- register-window\n459\t  \"§4.2 step 4: `{virt(site, s) V}` over the entry's `[s V]` pairs.\"\n460\t  [cx seg site regs]\n461\t  (into {} (map (fn [[s x]] [(virt seg site s) (canonical-value cx x)])) regs))\n462\t\n463\t\n464\t(defn- register-kframe\n465\t  \"§4.2 step 6, one frame independently: its own image row and A, `rd`\n466\t   from A, never the physical `:dest`.\"\n467\t  [cx f]\n468\t  (let [[seg pc] (image-pc :register (:kernel cx) (:space cx) (:return-pc f))]\n469\t    (safepoint! seg pc false)\n470\t    {:type :return :segment (:address seg) :pc pc\n471\t     :env (canonical-env cx seg pc (:frames f) (:store-of f))\n472\t     :window (register-window cx seg (dec pc) (:regs f))\n473\t     :rd (code/rd (inst seg (dec pc)))}))\n474\t\n475\t\n476\t(defn- lift-register\n477\t  [cx entry]\n478\t  (let [[seg pc] (image-pc :register (:kernel cx) (:space cx) (:pc entry))\n479\t        tail? (= :return-result (:resume-mode entry))\n480\t        _ (safepoint! seg pc tail?)\n481\t        k (mapv #(register-kframe cx %) (:continuation entry))]\n482\t    (if tail?\n483\t      {:segment (:address seg) :site (dec pc) :k k :deliver {:deliver :return}}\n484\t      {:segment (:address seg) :pc pc\n485\t       :env (canonical-env cx seg pc (:frames entry) (:store-of entry))\n486\t       :window (register-window cx seg (dec pc) (:regs entry))\n487\t       :k k\n488\t       :deliver {:deliver :rd :rd (code/rd (inst seg (dec pc)))}})))\n489\t\n490\t\n491\t(defn slices\n492\t  \"§4.3's K slices of operand stack `st` under return frames whose bases\n493\t   are `bases`, outermost first: `St[b(i-1), b(i))` per frame with\n494\t   `b(-1) = 0`, then the current activation's `St[b(m-1), n)`.\"\n495\t  [st bases]\n496\t  (let [bounds (into [0] bases)]\n497\t    (conj (mapv (fn [lo hi] (subvec st lo hi)) bounds (rest bounds))\n498\t          (subvec st (peek bounds) (count st)))))\n499\t\n500\t\n501\t(defn- slice-window\n502\t  \"`(zipmap (stack-layout (dec pc)) slice)`; the two have equal length.\"\n503\t  [cx seg pc slice]\n504\t  (let [layout (stack-layout seg (dec pc))]\n505\t    (when-not (= (count layout) (count slice))\n506\t      (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :stack-layout {:yin.k/pc pc :yin.k/layout layout :yin.k/slice (count slice)}))\n507\t    (zipmap layout (map #(canonical-value cx %) slice))))\n508\t\n509\t\n510\t(defn- stack-site\n511\t  \"`[segment site]`: the A pc of the parking instruction before absolute\n512\t   stack resume pc `abs`.\"\n513\t  [cx abs]\n514\t  (let [[seg rel] (image-pc :stack (:kernel cx) (:space cx) abs)]\n515\t    [seg (h->a seg (dec rel))]))\n516\t\n517\t\n518\t(defn- lift-stack\n519\t  [cx entry]\n520\t  (let [st (vec (:stack entry))\n521\t        frames (vec (:continuation entry))\n522\t        bases (mapv :stack-base frames)\n523\t        _ (when-not (and (every? integer? bases) (apply <= 0 (conj bases (count st))))\n524\t            (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :stack-base {:yin.k/bases bases}))\n525\t        parts (slices st bases)\n526\t        [seg site] (stack-site cx (:pc entry))\n527\t        tail? (code/tail-call? (inst seg site))\n528\t        _ (safepoint! seg (inc site) tail?)\n529\t        k (mapv (fn [f slice]\n530\t                  (let [[fseg fsite] (stack-site cx (:return-pc f))\n531\t                        pc (inc fsite)]\n532\t                    (safepoint! fseg pc false)\n533\t                    {:type :return :segment (:address fseg) :pc pc\n534\t                     :env (canonical-env cx fseg pc (:frames f) (:store-of f))\n535\t                     :window (slice-window cx fseg pc slice)\n536\t                     :rd (code/rd (inst fseg fsite))}))\n537\t                frames parts)]\n538\t    (if tail?\n539\t      ;; frozen §2.4: the current frames, store-of and slice are dead\n540\t      {:segment (:address seg) :site site :k k :deliver {:deliver :return}}\n541\t      (let [pc (inc site)]\n542\t        {:segment (:address seg) :pc pc\n543\t         :env (canonical-env cx seg pc (:frames entry) (:store-of entry))\n544\t         :window (slice-window cx seg pc (peek parts))\n545\t         :k k\n546\t         :deliver {:deliver :rd :rd (code/rd (inst seg site))}}))))\n547\t\n548\t\n549\t(defn lift-state\n550\t  \"A kernel's wait entry, parked record or reified payload as the\n551\t   canonical act or tail payload (§4.2, §4.3), engine fields carried.\"\n552\t  [cx entry]\n553\t  (let [fields (apply dissoc entry kernel-keys)]\n554\t    (merge (cond-> fields (contains? fields :datom) (update :datom #(canonical-value cx %)))\n555\t           (if (= :stack (:kind cx)) (lift-stack cx entry) (lift-register cx entry)))))\n556\t\n557\t\n558\t(defn lift-context\n559\t  \"The lift context of kernel `k` over `space`; positional closures and\n560\t   kernel continuations are reminted under `owner`.\"\n561\t  [k space owner]\n562\t  (assoc {} :kind (kernel-kind k) :kernel k :space space :free-env (or (:free-env k) {}) :owner owner))\n563\t\n564\t\n565\t;; =============================================================================\n566\t;; Lower: a canonical act or tail into a kernel\n567\t;; =============================================================================\n568\t\n569\t(defn- lower-frames\n570\t  [lx seg pc env]\n571\t  (mapv (fn [f] (mapv #(positional-value lx %) f)) (frames-of seg pc env)))\n572\t\n573\t\n574\t(defn- payload-segment\n575\t  [lx p]\n576\t  (segment-of (:space lx) (address-of lx (:segment p))))\n577\t\n578\t\n579\t(defn- lower-regs\n580\t  \"`(sort-by first [[phys(v) V] …])` over window `w`.\"\n581\t  [lx seg site w]\n582\t  (vec (sort-by first (map (fn [[x v]] [(phys seg site x) (positional-value lx v)]) w))))\n583\t\n584\t\n585\t(defn- register-k\n586\t  [lx k]\n587\t  (mapv (fn [f]\n588\t          (let [seg (payload-segment lx f)\n589\t                off (offset :register (:kernel lx) seg)\n590\t                pc (:pc f)\n591\t                regs (lower-regs lx seg (dec pc) (:window f))\n592\t                store-of (engine/env-store-of (:env f))]\n593\t            (cond-> {:site-pc (+ off pc -1) :return-pc (+ off pc)\n594\t                     :frames (lower-frames lx seg pc (:env f))\n595\t                     :regs regs :live (mapv first regs)\n596\t                     :dest (phys seg (dec pc) (:rd f))}\n597\t              (some? store-of) (assoc :store-of store-of))))\n598\t        k))\n599\t\n600\t\n601\t(defn- lower-register\n602\t  [lx p fill]\n603\t  (let [k (:kernel lx)\n604\t        seg (payload-segment lx p)\n605\t        off (offset :register k seg)\n606\t        common (cond-> {:segment (:segment k) :format register/format-tag :hash (:hash k)\n607\t                        :image (:register-id seg) :continuation (register-k lx (:k p))}\n608\t                 (< 1 (count (:images k))) (assoc :images (:images k)))]\n609\t    (if (contains? p :site)\n610\t      (cond-> (merge common {:site-pc (+ off (:site p)) :pc (+ off (:site p) 1)\n611\t                             :frames (get fill :frames []) :regs [] :live [] :dest nil\n612\t                             :resume-mode :return-result})\n613\t        (some? (:store-of fill)) (assoc :store-of (:store-of fill)))\n614\t      (let [pc (:pc p)\n615\t            regs (lower-regs lx seg (dec pc) (:window p))\n616\t            store-of (engine/env-store-of (:env p))]\n617\t        (cond-> (merge common {:site-pc (+ off pc -1) :pc (+ off pc)\n618\t                               :frames (lower-frames lx seg pc (:env p))\n619\t                               :regs regs :live (mapv first regs)\n620\t                               :dest (phys seg (dec pc) (get-in p [:deliver :rd]))\n621\t                               :resume-mode :write-result})\n622\t          (some? store-of) (assoc :store-of store-of))))))\n623\t\n624\t\n625\t(defn- layout-slice\n626\t  \"`(mapv W (stack-layout (dec pc)))`, every member present in W.\"\n627\t  [lx seg pc w]\n628\t  (mapv (fn [x]\n629\t          (when-not (contains? w x)\n630\t            (refuse! :yin.k/undecodable :window-missing {:yin.k/pc pc :yin.k/register x}))\n631\t          (positional-value lx (get w x)))\n632\t        (stack-layout seg (dec pc))))\n633\t\n634\t\n635\t(defn- stack-k\n636\t  \"`[frames stack]`: each return frame's slice appended in order, its base\n637\t   the cumulative length through its own slice (§4.3), `b(i) = b(i-1) +\n638\t   |slice(i)|`.\"\n639\t  [lx k]\n640\t  (reduce (fn [[frames st] f]\n641\t            (let [seg (payload-segment lx f)\n642\t                  pc (:pc f)\n643\t                  st (into st (layout-slice lx seg pc (:window f)))\n644\t                  store-of (engine/env-store-of (:env f))]\n645\t              [(conj frames (cond-> {:return-pc (+ (offset :stack (:kernel lx) seg) (a->h seg (dec pc)) 1)\n646\t                                     :frames (lower-frames lx seg pc (:env f))\n647\t                                     :stack-base (count st)}\n648\t                              (some? store-of) (assoc :store-of store-of)))\n649\t               st]))\n650\t          [[] []]\n651\t          k))\n652\t\n653\t\n654\t(defn- lower-stack\n655\t  [lx p fill]\n656\t  (let [k (:kernel lx)\n657\t        seg (payload-segment lx p)\n658\t        off (offset :stack k seg)\n659\t        [frames st] (stack-k lx (:k p))\n660\t        common {:segment (:segment k) :format stack/format-tag :hash (:hash k) :image (:stack-id seg)\n661\t                :continuation frames}]\n662\t    (if (contains? p :site)\n663\t      (cond-> (merge common {:pc (+ off (a->h seg (:site p)) 1) :resume-mode :return-result\n664\t                             :stack st :frames (get fill :frames [])})\n665\t        (some? (:store-of fill)) (assoc :store-of (:store-of fill)))\n666\t      (let [pc (:pc p)\n667\t            store-of (engine/env-store-of (:env p))]\n668\t        (cond-> (merge common {:pc (+ off (a->h seg (dec pc)) 1)\n669\t                               :frames (lower-frames lx seg pc (:env p))\n670\t                               :stack (into st (layout-slice lx seg pc (:window p)))})\n671\t          (some? store-of) (assoc :store-of store-of))))))\n672\t\n673\t\n674\t(def ^:private canonical-keys\n675\t  #{:segment :pc :site :env :window :k :deliver :type :id})\n676\t\n677\t\n678\t(defn lower-state\n679\t  \"Canonical act or tail payload `p` as the receiving kernel's entry\n680\t   (§4.2, §4.3), engine fields carried. `fill` gives a lowered tail's\n681\t   `:frames`/`:store-of` (default `[]`/nil), which the return transition\n682\t   replaces unread (§5.6 item 8).\"\n683\t  ([lx p] (lower-state lx p {}))\n684\t  ([lx p fill]\n685\t   (let [fields (apply dissoc p canonical-keys)]\n686\t     (merge (cond-> fields (contains? fields :datom) (update :datom #(positional-value lx %)))\n687\t            (if (= :stack (:kind lx)) (lower-stack lx p fill) (lower-register lx p fill))))))\n688\t\n689\t\n690\t(defn lower-context\n691\t  \"The lower context into kernel `k` over `space`; `address` maps a\n692\t   payload's or closure's `:segment` to A (default: it is A).\"\n693\t  ([k space] (lower-context k space identity))\n694\t  ([k space address]\n695\t   (assoc {} :kind (kernel-kind k) :kernel k :space space :free-env (or (:free-env k) {})\n696\t          :owner (:owner k) :address address)))\n697\t\n698\t\n699\t;; =============================================================================\n700\t;; Tasks\n701\t;; =============================================================================\n702\t\n703\t(defn- holds-procedure?\n704\t  \"Whether kernel value `x` is or holds, through guest collections, a\n705\t   closure or continuation.\"\n706\t  [x]\n707\t  (cond\n708\t    (or (values/closure? x) (values/continuation? x)) true\n709\t    (values/host-typed? x) false\n710\t    (map? x) (boolean (some holds-procedure? (concat (keys x) (vals x))))\n711\t    (coll? x) (boolean (some holds-procedure? x))\n712\t    :else false))\n713\t\n714\t\n715\t(defn canonical-task\n716\t  \"Quiescent kernel `k` presented as semantic-register machine `template`\n717\t   (a fresh machine of the same composition) for `rows/lift-task`: code\n718\t   by A from `space`, every wait, parked record and value reconstructed\n719\t   through the layout; resources, stores and counters carried. A running\n720\t   kernel is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; install children are not carried\n721\t   by this phase's layouts; a closure or continuation in the free\n722\t   environment has no finite canonical form (§6.3).\"\n723\t  [k space template]\n724\t  (when (engine/active-continuation? k)\n725\t    (refuse! :yin.k/not-at-safepoint nil {:yin.k/pc (:pc k)}))\n726\t  (when (seq (:installs k))\n727\t    (refuse! :yin.k/unsupported :installs {:yin.k/names (vec (keys (:installs k)))}))\n728\t  (when-let [sym (some (fn [[sym x]] (when (holds-procedure? x) sym)) (sort-by (comp str key) (:free-env k)))]\n729\t    (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :free-env-value {:yin.k/key sym}))\n730\t  (let [cx (lift-context k space (:owner template))\n731\t        conv #(canonical-value cx %)\n732\t        parked (into {} (map (fn [[id rec]] [id (assoc (lift-state cx rec) :type :parked-continuation :id id)]))\n733\t                     (:parked k))\n734\t        value (:value k)\n735\t        parked-value? (and (map? value) (= :parked-continuation (:type value)) (contains? parked (:id value)))\n736\t        code (into {} (map (fn [[a s]] [a (assoc {} :vector (:vector s) :address a :analysis (:analysis s))]))\n737\t                   (:segments space))]\n738\t    (assoc template\n739\t           :code code\n740\t           :code-aliases (into {} (map (fn [a] [a a])) (keys code))\n741\t           :program nil :control nil :window {} :k nil\n742\t           :resources (:resources k)\n743\t           :capability-secret (:capability-secret k)\n744\t           :free-env (:free-env k)\n745\t           :env (or (:free-env k) {})\n746\t           :store (conv (:store k))\n747\t           :module-stores (conv (or (:module-stores k) {}))\n748\t           :parked parked\n749\t           :wait-set (mapv #(lift-state cx %) (:wait-set k))\n750\t           :ready-queue (:ready-queue k)\n751\t           :value (if parked-value? (get parked (:id value)) (conv value))\n752\t           :id-counter (:id-counter k)\n753\t           :installs {}\n754\t           :halted? (:halted? k)\n755\t           :blocked? (:blocked? k))))\n756\t\n757\t\n758\t(defn lift-task\n759\t  \"§7.1 for a de Bruijn kernel: `rows/lift-task` over its canonical\n760\t   presentation. `template` is a fresh semantic-register machine of the\n761\t   kernel's composition; opts as rows/lift-task's.\"\n762\t  [k space template serve! opts]\n763\t  (rows/lift-task (canonical-task k space template) serve! opts))\n764\t\n765\t\n766\t(defn attach-space\n767\t  \"Kernel `k` holding the derived image of every segment of `space`, in\n768\t   A order (§7.2: derive and attach the image for each A).\"\n769\t  [k space]\n770\t  (let [kind (kernel-kind k)]\n771\t    (reduce (fn [k a]\n772\t              (let [seg (segment-of space a)]\n773\t                (if (= :stack kind)\n774\t                  (stack/attach-image k (:stack seg) vm/stack-contract)\n775\t                  (register/attach-image k (:register seg) vm/register-contract))))\n776\t            k\n777\t            (sort-by str (keys (:segments space))))))\n778\t\n779\t\n780\t(defn machine-space\n781\t  \"The space of the A vectors semantic-register machine `m` holds, and\n782\t   the map from its segment ids to A.\"\n783\t  [m]\n784\t  [(space (map :vector (vals (:code m))))\n785\t   (fn [seg] (or (get-in m [:code seg :address]) (when (contains? (:code-aliases m) seg) seg)))])\n786\t\n787\t\n788\t(defn kernel-task\n789\t  \"Step 12 for a de Bruijn receiver: semantic-register machine `m` (what\n790\t   `rows/lower-task` restored) as fresh kernel `k` of the same\n791\t   composition: every image derived and attached, the free environment\n792\t   set, every wait, parked record and value lowered through the layout.\n793\t   Delivers nothing, runs nothing.\"\n794\t  [m k]\n795\t  (let [[sp address] (machine-space m)\n796\t        k (attach-space k sp)\n797\t        lx (lower-context k sp address)\n798\t        conv #(positional-value lx %)\n799\t        parked (into {} (map (fn [[id rec]] [id (assoc (lower-state lx rec) :type :parked-continuation :id id)]))\n800\t                     (:parked m))\n801\t        value (:value m)\n802\t        parked-value? (and (map? value) (= :parked-continuation (:type value)) (contains? parked (:id value)))]\n803\t    (assoc k\n804\t           :resources (:resources m)\n805\t           :free-env (:free-env m)\n806\t           :store (conv (:store m))\n807\t           :module-stores (conv (or (:module-stores m) {}))\n808\t           :parked parked\n809\t           :wait-set (mapv #(lower-state lx %) (:wait-set m))\n810\t           :ready-queue []\n811\t           :value (if parked-value? (get parked (:id value)) (conv value))\n812\t           :id-counter (:id-counter m)\n813\t           :halted? (:halted? m)\n814\t           :blocked? (:blocked? m))))\n815\t\n816\t\n817\t(defn- lexical-names\n818\t  \"The names the chain binds at `pc` of `v`.\"\n819\t  [v pc]\n820\t  (let [bodies (code/body-ranges v)]\n821\t    (loop [b (code/body-index bodies pc) out #{}]\n822\t      (if-let [owner (:owner (nth bodies b))]\n823\t        (recur (code/body-index bodies owner) (into out (nth (nth v owner) 2)))\n824\t        out))))\n825\t\n826\t\n827\t(declare state-prim)\n828\t\n829\t\n830\t(defn- value-prim\n831\t  \"The path to the first `:prim` row carrier value `x` reaches as kernel\n832\t   payload data: through guest collections, a closure's lexical bindings\n833\t   (its positional frames) and a capture's state; or nil.\"\n834\t  [code x path]\n835\t  (when (and (vector? x) (= :row (first x)))\n836\t    (let [n (second x) ss (:slots n)]\n837\t      (case (:kind n)\n838\t        :prim path\n839\t        (:vec :list :set :map) (some (fn [[i y]] (value-prim code y (conj path i))) (map-indexed vector ss))\n840\t        :closure (let [[a entry _ bindings] ss\n841\t                       v (get code a)\n842\t                       owner (when v (some (fn [pc]\n843\t                                             (let [t (nth v pc)]\n844\t                                               (when (and (= :closure (nth t 0)) (= entry (nth t 3))) pc)))\n845\t                                           (range (count v))))\n846\t                       names (if owner (lexical-names v owner) #{})]\n847\t                   (some (fn [[s y]] (when (contains? names s) (value-prim code y (conj path s))))\n848\t                         (partition 2 (:slots bindings))))\n849\t        :reified (state-prim code (first ss) (conj path 0))\n850\t        nil))))\n851\t\n852\t\n853\t(defn- frame-prim\n854\t  \"A frame's window and lexical bindings (§4.4: the free part is the\n855\t   kernel's :free-env, never payload data).\"\n856\t  [code a pc e w path]\n857\t  (let [names (lexical-names (get code a) pc)]\n858\t    (or (some (fn [[r y]] (value-prim code y (conj path :window r))) (partition 2 (:slots w)))\n859\t        (some (fn [[sym y]] (when (contains? names sym) (value-prim code y (conj path :env sym))))\n860\t              (partition 2 (:slots (first (:slots e))))))))\n861\t\n862\t\n863\t(defn- state-prim\n864\t  [code n path]\n865\t  (let [kframes (fn [k]\n866\t                  (some (fn [[i f]]\n867\t                          (let [[a pc e w] (:slots f)]\n868\t                            (frame-prim code a pc e w (conj path :k i))))\n869\t                        (map-indexed vector (:slots k))))\n870\t        [a pc e w k] (:slots n)]\n871\t    (case (:kind n)\n872\t      :act (or (frame-prim code a pc e w path) (kframes k))\n873\t      :tail (kframes e)\n874\t      nil)))\n875\t\n876\t\n877\t(defn- row-procedure\n878\t  \"The path to the first closure or continuation row carrier value `x`\n879\t   holds, through guest collections; or nil.\"\n880\t  [x path]\n881\t  (when (and (vector? x) (= :row (first x)))\n882\t    (let [n (second x)]\n883\t      (case (:kind n)\n884\t        (:closure :reified) path\n885\t        (:vec :list :set :map) (some (fn [[i y]] (row-procedure y (conj path i))) (map-indexed vector (:slots n)))\n886\t        nil))))\n887\t\n888\t\n889\t(defn- free-env-procedure\n890\t  \"§6.3: a de Bruijn kernel resolves free names through its one\n891\t   `free-env` while a canonical closure snapshots its `env`, so a closure\n892\t   or continuation held in the free environment has no finite canonical\n893\t   form there. The path `[:free-env sym …]` to the first such value of\n894\t   decoded task `task`, or nil.\"\n895\t  [task]\n896\t  (some (fn [[sym x]] (row-procedure x [:free-env sym]))\n897\t        (partition 2 (:slots (nth (:slots task) 5)))))\n898\t\n899\t\n900\t(defn- image-collision\n901\t  \"§4.1: `[ident #{A …}]` for the first image of kind `kind` two carried A\n902\t   derive, or nil. A de Bruijn kernel attaches one image once, so it\n903\t   cannot hold both segments; no enumeration order selects an A. A\n904\t   vector that derives no image is left to the carrier's own checks.\"\n905\t  [code kind]\n906\t  (let [ident (fn [a v]\n907\t                (try\n908\t                  (if (= :stack kind)\n909\t                    (dcode/image-hash (:image (derive/stack-image v)))\n910\t                    (rcode/register-hash (derive/register-image v)))\n911\t                  (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _ [::underivable a])))\n912\t        by-image (reduce (fn [acc [a v]] (update acc (ident a v) (fnil conj #{}) a)) {} code)]\n913\t    (some (fn [[i as]] (when (< 1 (count as)) [i as])) (sort-by (comp str key) by-image))))\n914\t\n915\t\n916\t(defn lower-defect\n917\t  \"What a kernel of `kind` cannot hold, judged on decoded task `task`\n918\t   before anything is attached: two carried A of one image (§4.1), install\n919\t   children (not carried by this phase's layouts), a closure or\n920\t   continuation in the free environment (§6.3), and for the register\n921\t   kernel a primitive function in the payload data of a wait or parked\n922\t   state (a window, a lexical binding, or reached through them), which\n923\t   its restore refuses as `:continuation-registers` (`vm/machine-data?`).\n924\t   nil when it can.\"\n925\t  [task kind]\n926\t  (let [slots (:slots task)\n927\t        code (nth slots 11)\n928\t        collision (image-collision code kind)\n929\t        free (free-env-procedure task)]\n930\t    (cond\n931\t      collision\n932\t      (assoc {} :yin.k/status :yin.k/unsupported :yin.k/kind :ambiguous-image\n933\t             :yin.k/image (first collision) :yin.k/segments (second collision))\n934\t\n935\t      (seq (nth slots 12))\n936\t      (assoc {} :yin.k/status :yin.k/unsupported :yin.k/kind :installs)\n937\t\n938\t      free\n939\t      (assoc {} :yin.k/status :yin.k/unsupported :yin.k/kind :free-env-value :yin.k/path free)\n940\t\n941\t      (= :register kind)\n942\t      (when-let [path (or (some (fn [[i w]] (state-prim code (first (:slots w)) [:frames i]))\n943\t                                (map-indexed vector (nth slots 13)))\n944\t                          (some (fn [[p st]] (state-prim code st [:parked p]))\n945\t                                (partition 2 (:slots (nth slots 8)))))]\n946\t        (assoc {} :yin.k/status :yin.k/unsupported :yin.k/kind :continuation-registers :yin.k/path path))\n947\t\n948\t      :else nil)))\n949\t\n950\t\n951\t(defn lower-task\n952\t  \"§7.2 into a de Bruijn kernel: decode `wire`, refuse what kernel `k`\n953\t   cannot hold (`lower-defect`) before any attach, then `rows/lower-task`\n954\t   into `scratch` (a fresh semantic-register machine of the same\n955\t   composition: the carrier's steps 1-11 and restore) and `kernel-task`\n956\t   into `k`. Throws the first refusal as ex-data.\"\n957\t  ([scratch k wire attach!] (lower-task scratch k wire attach! {}))\n958\t  ([scratch k wire attach! opts]\n959\t   (let [task (rows/decode wire (merge rows/default-budget (:budget opts)))]\n960\t     (when (= :task (:kind task))\n961\t       (when-let [d (lower-defect task (kernel-kind k))]\n962\t         (throw (ex-info \"Layout refused\" d))))\n963\t     (kernel-task (rows/lower-task scratch wire attach! opts) k))))\n964\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f7716a8d-bcb9-4b49-9ad0-ed6a5daed388","uuid":"aecb6d1f-8739-4bb0-bc9d-449e7065448a","timestamp":"2026-10-13T09:47:19.185Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7plan/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc","content":"(ns yin.vm.semantic-register.layout\n  \"Phase 6c (docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md §4):\n   the de Bruijn register and stack kernels reconstruct the canonical\n   frame of the register machine through their layouts, and lower it\n   back.\n\n   Static half, per segment A: `segment` derives R(A) and H(A) and reads\n   off the register pairing (`phys`, the restricted inverse `virt`, the\n   kernel's `live` as live-out with tail boundaries `[]`), the stack\n   correspondence (`h->a`, `a->h`, every `:push` removed) and the operand\n   layout `stack-layout` (the values pushed and not yet consumed while\n   control is inside a pc's expression). A `space` indexes segments by\n   the kernels' image identities, R and H, which are checksums: A is the\n   only code identity.\n\n   Dynamic half: `lift-state` reads a kernel's wait entry, parked record\n   or reified payload as the canonical `act` or `tail` payload the\n   semantic-register evaluator mints (`{:segment A :pc :env :window :k\n   :deliver}`, `{:segment A :site :k :deliver}`, engine fields carried);\n   `lower-state` is its inverse. The register kernel's return\n   destinations are recovered from A, never from the physical `:dest`;\n   the stack kernel's operand stack is sliced `[previous-base, base)` per\n   frame and lowered with cumulative bases. `E` is the free environment\n   merged under the chain's positional frames (§4.4); the inverse reads\n   each frame back from `E` by the chain, never the free environment.\n\n   Task level: `canonical-task` presents a quiescent kernel as a\n   semantic-register machine for `yin.vm.ucf.rows/lift-task`;\n   `kernel-task` restores a machine `rows/lower-task` produced into a\n   fresh kernel; `lift-task` and `lower-task` compose them with the row\n   carrier, the lower refusing what a kernel cannot hold before any\n   stream is attached. A layout never runs code, never fills a binding\n   from the receiver and never invents a safepoint (§4.5).\"\n  (:require [dao.jing :as jing]\n            [yin.vm.debruijn-code :as dcode]\n            [yin.vm.debruijn-register-code :as rcode]\n            [yin.vm.debruijn.register :as register]\n            [yin.vm.debruijn.stack :as stack]\n            [yin.vm :as vm]\n            [yin.vm.engine :as engine]\n            [yin.vm.module :as module]\n            [yin.vm.semantic-register.analysis :as analysis]\n            [yin.vm.semantic-register.code :as code]\n            [yin.vm.semantic-register.derive :as derive]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.rows :as rows]\n            [yin.vm.values :as values]))\n\n\n(defn- refuse!\n  [status kind data]\n  (throw (ex-info \"Layout refused\" (merge data (assoc {} :yin.k/status status :yin.k/kind kind)))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Static half: one segment\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- operands\n  \"The register operands of an A tuple, destination first, then its uses\n   in table order.\"\n  [t]\n  (into (if-some [r (code/rd t)] [r] []) (code/uses t)))\n\n\n(defn- register-operands\n  \"The `:reg`/`:regs` operands of an R tuple in table order (the `:live`\n   slot is `:data`, never paired).\"\n  [t]\n  (into []\n        (mapcat (fn [[[_ kind] x]] (case kind :reg [x] :regs x [])))\n        (map vector (get rcode/opcode-table (nth t 0)) (rest t))))\n\n\n(defn register-pairs\n  \"`[pc virtual physical]` for every register operand of every pc: A's\n   tuple and R's name their registers in the same slots (phase-5 §2.4).\"\n  [v image]\n  (into []\n        (mapcat (fn [pc]\n                  (map (fn [x s] [pc x s])\n                       (operands (nth v pc))\n                       (register-operands (nth (:instructions image) pc)))))\n        (range (count v))))\n\n\n(defn- phys-maps\n  \"Per body, `{virtual physical}` read off the pairs; the first pc that\n   names a virtual wins (register-pairing checks they agree).\"\n  [bodies pairs]\n  (reduce (fn [acc [pc x s]]\n            (let [b (code/body-index bodies pc)]\n              (if (contains? (nth acc b) x) acc (assoc-in acc [b x] s))))\n          (vec (repeat (count bodies) {}))\n          pairs))\n\n\n(defn correspondence\n  \"§4.3 static item 1: H with every `:push` removed corresponds one to one,\n   in order, with A. Answers `{:a->h [h-pc …] :h->a {h-pc a-pc}}`.\"\n  [h]\n  (reduce (fn [acc hpc]\n            (if (= :push (nth (nth h hpc) 0))\n              acc\n              (-> acc\n                  (update :a->h conj hpc)\n                  (assoc-in [:h->a hpc] (count (:a->h acc))))))\n          {:a->h [] :h->a {}}\n          (range (count h))))\n\n\n(def ^:private pushing-ops\n  \"The parse nodes whose children before the current one are pushed:\n   `:call` (the operator and earlier operands), `:ffi-call` (earlier\n   operands) and `:stream-put` (the target, while the value runs).\"\n  #{:call :ffi-call :stream-put})\n\n\n(defn- stack-layouts\n  \"§4.3 static item 2 for every pc of every body: `{pc [virtual …]}`,\n   bottom to top, over the parse tree's ancestors outermost first.\"\n  [v bodies]\n  (letfn [(walk\n            [out node pending]\n            (let [out (assoc out (:pc node) pending)\n                  pushes? (contains? pushing-ops (:op node))\n                  uses (when pushes? (code/uses (nth v (:pc node))))]\n              (reduce (fn [out [i child]]\n                        (walk out child (if pushes? (into pending (take i uses)) pending)))\n                      out\n                      (map-indexed vector (:children node)))))]\n    (reduce (fn [out body] (walk out (:expr body) [])) {} bodies)))\n\n\n(defn segment\n  \"The static facts of one admitted A vector `v`, for both kernels.\"\n  [v]\n  (let [bodies (code/parse v)\n        r (derive/register-image v)\n        h (:image (derive/stack-image v))\n        pairs (register-pairs v r)]\n    (merge {:vector v\n            :address (jing/segment-key v)\n            :bodies bodies\n            :analysis (analysis/analyze v)\n            :register r\n            :register-id (rcode/register-hash r)\n            :stack h\n            :stack-id (dcode/image-hash h)\n            :pairs pairs\n            :phys (phys-maps bodies pairs)\n            :layouts (stack-layouts v bodies)}\n           (correspondence h))))\n\n\n(defn space\n  \"Segments by A, and A by each kernel's image identity. `segments` is a\n   seq of `segment` results or of A vectors.\"\n  [segments]\n  (reduce (fn [acc s]\n            (let [s (if (map? s) s (segment s))\n                  a (:address s)]\n              (-> acc\n                  (assoc-in [:segments a] s)\n                  (update-in [:register (:register-id s)] (fnil conj #{}) a)\n                  (update-in [:stack (:stack-id s)] (fnil conj #{}) a))))\n          {:segments {} :register {} :stack {}}\n          segments))\n\n\n(defn segment-of\n  \"The segment A names in `space`.\"\n  [space a]\n  (or (get-in space [:segments a])\n      (refuse! :yin.k/unsatisfied :segment {:yin.k/segment a})))\n\n\n(defn- image-segment\n  \"The segment whose `kind` image is `ident`: an identity is a checksum,\n   so two A deriving one image are ambiguous and refused.\"\n  [space kind ident]\n  (let [as (get-in space [kind ident])]\n    (cond\n      (empty? as) (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :unknown-image {:yin.k/image ident})\n      (< 1 (count as)) (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :ambiguous-image {:yin.k/image ident})\n      :else (segment-of space (first as)))))\n\n\n(defn- inst\n  [seg pc]\n  (nth (:vector seg) pc))\n\n\n(defn- body-of\n  [seg pc]\n  (code/body-index (:bodies seg) pc))\n\n\n(defn phys\n  \"`phys(body(pc), x)`: the physical register R names for virtual `x` in\n   the body holding `pc`.\"\n  [seg pc x]\n  (let [s (get (nth (:phys seg) (body-of seg pc)) x)]\n    (when (nil? s) (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :register-layout {:yin.k/pc pc :yin.k/register x}))\n    s))\n\n\n(defn live-out\n  \"`live-out*(p)`: the union of `L(q)` over p's actual successors (empty\n   for a tail call).\"\n  [seg p]\n  (reduce (fn [acc q] (into acc (analysis/live (:analysis seg) q)))\n          #{}\n          (analysis/successors (:vector seg) p)))\n\n\n(defn register-live\n  \"§5.6 item 3: the `live` operand R must carry at boundary `p`,\n   `(sort (map phys (disj live-out*(p) rd)))`, the destination removed as\n   a virtual id before mapping; `[]` at a tail call.\"\n  [seg p]\n  (vec (sort (map #(phys seg p %) (disj (live-out seg p) (code/rd (inst seg p)))))))\n\n\n(defn virt\n  \"The restricted inverse of `phys` at boundary `p`: the virtual of\n   `live-out*(p)` held in physical `s`. A slot outside that image (only a\n   tampered entry names one) reads as the one virtual of the body R maps\n   there, so the frame validator judges the window; an ambiguous slot is\n   refused.\"\n  [seg p s]\n  (let [m (nth (:phys seg) (body-of seg p))\n        live (filterv #(= s (get m %)) (sort (live-out seg p)))\n        named (filterv #(= s (get m %)) (sort (keys m)))]\n    (cond\n      (= 1 (count live)) (first live)\n      (and (empty? live) (= 1 (count named))) (first named)\n      :else (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :register-layout {:yin.k/pc p :yin.k/register s}))))\n\n\n(defn stack-layout\n  \"§4.3: the virtual registers pushed and not yet consumed while control\n   is inside the expression at A pc `p`, bottom to top.\"\n  [seg p]\n  (get (:layouts seg) p))\n\n\n(defn chain\n  \"The closure pcs owning the body that holds `pc`, outermost first.\"\n  [seg pc]\n  (loop [b (body-of seg pc) out ()]\n    (if-let [owner (:owner (nth (:bodies seg) b))]\n      (recur (body-of seg owner) (conj out owner))\n      (vec out))))\n\n\n(defn- params\n  [seg c]\n  (nth (inst seg c) 2))\n\n\n(defn env-of\n  \"§4.4 forward: `E` at `pc` from the positional frames `F` (outermost\n   first, values already canonical), the task's free environment and the\n   activation's module store.\"\n  [seg pc free-env frames store-of]\n  (let [cs (chain seg pc)]\n    (when-not (= (count cs) (count frames))\n      (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :frame-depth {:yin.k/pc pc :yin.k/depth (count frames)}))\n    (cond-> (reduce (fn [e [c f]] (merge e (engine/bind-params (params seg c) f)))\n                    (or free-env {})\n                    (map vector cs frames))\n      (some? store-of) (assoc engine/store-of-key store-of))))\n\n\n(defn frames-of\n  \"§4.4 inverse: `F[i] = (mapv #(get E %) params(cᵢ))` over the chain at\n   `pc`. An outer value a shadowing inner binder hides reads as the inner\n   one; the canonical frame never held it.\"\n  [seg pc env]\n  (mapv (fn [c] (mapv #(get env %) (params seg c))) (chain seg pc)))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Kernel dispatch\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn kernel-kind\n  \"`:stack` or `:register`, by the kernel's link format.\"\n  [k]\n  (let [f (when (satisfies? module/IModuleKernel k) (:format (module/link-format k)))]\n    (cond\n      (= stack/format-tag f) :stack\n      (= register/format-tag f) :register\n      :else (refuse! :yin.k/unsupported :kernel {:yin.k/format f}))))\n\n\n(defn- image-pc\n  \"`[segment rel-pc]` of absolute kernel pc `pc`.\"\n  [kind k space pc]\n  (let [[ident rel] (or (if (= :stack kind) (stack/image-pc k pc) (register/image-pc k pc))\n                        (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :unknown-image {:yin.k/pc pc}))]\n    [(image-segment space kind ident) rel]))\n\n\n(defn offset\n  \"The offset of segment `seg`'s image in kernel `k`'s code space.\"\n  [kind k seg]\n  (let [ident (if (= :stack kind) (:stack-id seg) (:register-id seg))]\n    (or (if (= :stack kind) (stack/absolute-pc k [ident 0]) (register/absolute-pc k [ident 0]))\n        (refuse! :yin.k/unsatisfied :image-not-attached {:yin.k/segment (:address seg)}))))\n\n\n(def ^:private parking-ops\n  \"A1: the instructions a resume pc may follow, besides a non-tail call.\"\n  #{:stream-next :stream-put :ffi-call :park :current-continuation})\n\n\n(defn- safepoint!\n  \"Refuse `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` unless the A instruction before `pc`\n   parks (a tail call for a tail state): a layout never invents a\n   safepoint (§4.5).\"\n  [seg pc tail?]\n  (let [t (when (and (integer? pc) (< 0 pc) (<= pc (count (:vector seg)))) (inst seg (dec pc)))\n        op (when t (nth t 0))]\n    (when-not (and t\n                   (= (body-of seg (dec pc)) (body-of seg (min pc (dec (count (:vector seg))))))\n                   (if tail?\n                     (code/tail-call? t)\n                     (or (contains? parking-ops op) (and (= :call op) (not (code/tail-call? t))))))\n      (refuse! :yin.k/not-at-safepoint nil {:yin.k/pc pc}))))\n\n\n(defn- h->a\n  \"The A pc of H pc `hpc`; a `:push` has none and is no safepoint.\"\n  [seg hpc]\n  (or (get (:h->a seg) hpc) (refuse! :yin.k/not-at-safepoint nil {:yin.k/pc hpc})))\n\n\n(defn- a->h\n  [seg pc]\n  (nth (:a->h seg) pc))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Values: positional closures and kernel continuations, both ways\n;; =============================================================================\n;;\n;; A lift context `cx` is {:kind :kernel :space :free-env :owner}; a lower\n;; context `lx` adds :address, naming a canonical payload's or closure's\n;; :segment as A.\n\n(declare lift-state lower-state canonical-value positional-value)\n\n\n(defn- walk-value\n  \"`x` with its guest collections rebuilt around `f`, which converts\n   closures and continuations.\"\n  [f x]\n  (cond\n    (values/host-typed? x) (f x)\n    (map? x) (into {} (map (fn [[k v]] [(walk-value f k) (walk-value f v)])) x)\n    (vector? x) (mapv #(walk-value f %) x)\n    (set? x) (into #{} (map #(walk-value f %)) x)\n    (seq? x) (apply list (map #(walk-value f %) x))\n    :else x))\n\n\n(defn- canonical-closure\n  \"§4.4: a positional closure as the canonical `{:type :closure :params\n   :entry :segment :env}`: `params` from A's `:closure` at the owner of\n   the entry body, `env` by the forward rule over the closure's frames.\"\n  [cx c]\n  (let [[seg rel] (image-pc (:kind cx) (:kernel cx) (:space cx) (:body-pc c))\n        entry (if (= :stack (:kind cx)) (h->a seg rel) rel)\n        owner (:owner (nth (:bodies seg) (body-of seg entry)))\n        _ (when-not (and owner (= entry (nth (inst seg owner) 3)))\n            (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :closure-origin {:yin.k/entry entry}))\n        ps (params seg owner)]\n    (when-not (= (count ps) (:arity c))\n      (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :closure-binders {:yin.k/entry entry}))\n    {:type :closure :params ps :entry entry :segment (:address seg)\n     :env (env-of seg owner (:free-env cx)\n                  (mapv (fn [f] (mapv #(canonical-value cx %) f)) (:frames c))\n                  (:store-of c))}))\n\n\n(defn- canonical-value*\n  [cx x]\n  (cond\n    (values/closure? x) (values/closure (:owner cx) (canonical-closure cx (values/payload x)))\n    (values/continuation? x)\n    (values/continuation (:owner cx) (assoc (lift-state cx (values/payload x)) :type :reified-continuation))\n    :else x))\n\n\n(defn canonical-value\n  \"Kernel value `x` with every positional closure and kernel continuation\n   reconstructed canonically (cx: a lift context).\"\n  [cx x]\n  (walk-value #(canonical-value* cx %) x))\n\n\n(defn- address-of\n  [lx seg-id]\n  (let [a ((:address lx) seg-id)]\n    (when (nil? a) (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :unaddressed-segment {:yin.k/hint seg-id}))\n    a))\n\n\n(defn- positional-closure\n  \"The inverse of canonical-closure: the entry's absolute pc in the\n   receiving kernel, the frames read back from `env` by the owner's chain.\"\n  [lx c]\n  (let [seg (segment-of (:space lx) (address-of lx (:segment c)))\n        entry (:entry c)\n        owner (:owner (nth (:bodies seg) (body-of seg entry)))\n        store-of (engine/env-store-of (:env c))]\n    (when-not (and owner (= entry (nth (inst seg owner) 3)) (= (vec (:params c)) (vec (params seg owner))))\n      (refuse! :yin.k/undecodable :closure-binders {:yin.k/entry entry}))\n    (cond-> {:type :closure :arity (count (:params c))\n             :body-pc (+ (offset (:kind lx) (:kernel lx) seg) (if (= :stack (:kind lx)) (a->h seg entry) entry))\n             :frames (mapv (fn [f] (mapv #(positional-value lx %) f)) (frames-of seg owner (:env c)))}\n      (some? store-of) (assoc :store-of store-of))))\n\n\n(defn- positional-value*\n  [lx x]\n  (cond\n    (values/closure? x) (values/closure (:owner lx) (positional-closure lx (values/payload x)))\n    (values/continuation? x)\n    (values/continuation (:owner lx) (merge {:type :reified-continuation} (lower-state lx (values/payload x))))\n    :else x))\n\n\n(defn positional-value\n  \"Canonical value `x` with every closure and continuation lowered into\n   the receiving kernel (lx: a lower context).\"\n  [lx x]\n  (walk-value #(positional-value* lx %) x))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Lift: a kernel payload as the canonical act or tail\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(def ^:private kernel-keys\n  \"The register payload keys of either kernel; every other key of an\n   entry is the engine's and travels in the pending.\"\n  #{:segment :site-pc :pc :frames :regs :live :continuation :dest :resume-mode\n    :format :hash :image :images :store-of :stack})\n\n\n(defn- canonical-env\n  [cx seg pc frames store-of]\n  (env-of seg pc (:free-env cx) (mapv (fn [f] (mapv #(canonical-value cx %) f)) frames) store-of))\n\n\n(defn- register-window\n  \"§4.2 step 4: `{virt(site, s) V}` over the entry's `[s V]` pairs.\"\n  [cx seg site regs]\n  (into {} (map (fn [[s x]] [(virt seg site s) (canonical-value cx x)])) regs))\n\n\n(defn- register-kframe\n  \"§4.2 step 6, one frame independently: its own image row and A, `rd`\n   from A, never the physical `:dest`.\"\n  [cx f]\n  (let [[seg pc] (image-pc :register (:kernel cx) (:space cx) (:return-pc f))]\n    (safepoint! seg pc false)\n    {:type :return :segment (:address seg) :pc pc\n     :env (canonical-env cx seg pc (:frames f) (:store-of f))\n     :window (register-window cx seg (dec pc) (:regs f))\n     :rd (code/rd (inst seg (dec pc)))}))\n\n\n(defn- lift-register\n  [cx entry]\n  (let [[seg pc] (image-pc :register (:kernel cx) (:space cx) (:pc entry))\n        tail? (= :return-result (:resume-mode entry))\n        _ (safepoint! seg pc tail?)\n        k (mapv #(register-kframe cx %) (:continuation entry))]\n    (if tail?\n      {:segment (:address seg) :site (dec pc) :k k :deliver {:deliver :return}}\n      {:segment (:address seg) :pc pc\n       :env (canonical-env cx seg pc (:frames entry) (:store-of entry))\n       :window (register-window cx seg (dec pc) (:regs entry))\n       :k k\n       :deliver {:deliver :rd :rd (code/rd (inst seg (dec pc)))}})))\n\n\n(defn slices\n  \"§4.3's K slices of operand stack `st` under return frames whose bases\n   are `bases`, outermost first: `St[b(i-1), b(i))` per frame with\n   `b(-1) = 0`, then the current activation's `St[b(m-1), n)`.\"\n  [st bases]\n  (let [bounds (into [0] bases)]\n    (conj (mapv (fn [lo hi] (subvec st lo hi)) bounds (rest bounds))\n          (subvec st (peek bounds) (count st)))))\n\n\n(defn- slice-window\n  \"`(zipmap (stack-layout (dec pc)) slice)`; the two have equal length.\"\n  [cx seg pc slice]\n  (let [layout (stack-layout seg (dec pc))]\n    (when-not (= (count layout) (count slice))\n      (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :stack-layout {:yin.k/pc pc :yin.k/layout layout :yin.k/slice (count slice)}))\n    (zipmap layout (map #(canonical-value cx %) slice))))\n\n\n(defn- stack-site\n  \"`[segment site]`: the A pc of the parking instruction before absolute\n   stack resume pc `abs`.\"\n  [cx abs]\n  (let [[seg rel] (image-pc :stack (:kernel cx) (:space cx) abs)]\n    [seg (h->a seg (dec rel))]))\n\n\n(defn- lift-stack\n  [cx entry]\n  (let [st (vec (:stack entry))\n        frames (vec (:continuation entry))\n        bases (mapv :stack-base frames)\n        _ (when-not (and (every? integer? bases) (apply <= 0 (conj bases (count st))))\n            (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :stack-base {:yin.k/bases bases}))\n        parts (slices st bases)\n        [seg site] (stack-site cx (:pc entry))\n        tail? (code/tail-call? (inst seg site))\n        _ (safepoint! seg (inc site) tail?)\n        k (mapv (fn [f slice]\n                  (let [[fseg fsite] (stack-site cx (:return-pc f))\n                        pc (inc fsite)]\n                    (safepoint! fseg pc false)\n                    {:type :return :segment (:address fseg) :pc pc\n                     :env (canonical-env cx fseg pc (:frames f) (:store-of f))\n                     :window (slice-window cx fseg pc slice)\n                     :rd (code/rd (inst fseg fsite))}))\n                frames parts)]\n    (if tail?\n      ;; frozen §2.4: the current frames, store-of and slice are dead\n      {:segment (:address seg) :site site :k k :deliver {:deliver :return}}\n      (let [pc (inc site)]\n        {:segment (:address seg) :pc pc\n         :env (canonical-env cx seg pc (:frames entry) (:store-of entry))\n         :window (slice-window cx seg pc (peek parts))\n         :k k\n         :deliver {:deliver :rd :rd (code/rd (inst seg site))}}))))\n\n\n(defn lift-state\n  \"A kernel's wait entry, parked record or reified payload as the\n   canonical act or tail payload (§4.2, §4.3), engine fields carried.\"\n  [cx entry]\n  (let [fields (apply dissoc entry kernel-keys)]\n    (merge (cond-> fields (contains? fields :datom) (update :datom #(canonical-value cx %)))\n           (if (= :stack (:kind cx)) (lift-stack cx entry) (lift-register cx entry)))))\n\n\n(defn lift-context\n  \"The lift context of kernel `k` over `space`; positional closures and\n   kernel continuations are reminted under `owner`.\"\n  [k space owner]\n  (assoc {} :kind (kernel-kind k) :kernel k :space space :free-env (or (:free-env k) {}) :owner owner))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Lower: a canonical act or tail into a kernel\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- lower-frames\n  [lx seg pc env]\n  (mapv (fn [f] (mapv #(positional-value lx %) f)) (frames-of seg pc env)))\n\n\n(defn- payload-segment\n  [lx p]\n  (segment-of (:space lx) (address-of lx (:segment p))))\n\n\n(defn- lower-regs\n  \"`(sort-by first [[phys(v) V] …])` over window `w`.\"\n  [lx seg site w]\n  (vec (sort-by first (map (fn [[x v]] [(phys seg site x) (positional-value lx v)]) w))))\n\n\n(defn- register-k\n  [lx k]\n  (mapv (fn [f]\n          (let [seg (payload-segment lx f)\n                off (offset :register (:kernel lx) seg)\n                pc (:pc f)\n                regs (lower-regs lx seg (dec pc) (:window f))\n                store-of (engine/env-store-of (:env f))]\n            (cond-> {:site-pc (+ off pc -1) :return-pc (+ off pc)\n                     :frames (lower-frames lx seg pc (:env f))\n                     :regs regs :live (mapv first regs)\n                     :dest (phys seg (dec pc) (:rd f))}\n              (some? store-of) (assoc :store-of store-of))))\n        k))\n\n\n(defn- lower-register\n  [lx p fill]\n  (let [k (:kernel lx)\n        seg (payload-segment lx p)\n        off (offset :register k seg)\n        common (cond-> {:segment (:segment k) :format register/format-tag :hash (:hash k)\n                        :image (:register-id seg) :continuation (register-k lx (:k p))}\n                 (< 1 (count (:images k))) (assoc :images (:images k)))]\n    (if (contains? p :site)\n      (cond-> (merge common {:site-pc (+ off (:site p)) :pc (+ off (:site p) 1)\n                             :frames (get fill :frames []) :regs [] :live [] :dest nil\n                             :resume-mode :return-result})\n        (some? (:store-of fill)) (assoc :store-of (:store-of fill)))\n      (let [pc (:pc p)\n            regs (lower-regs lx seg (dec pc) (:window p))\n            store-of (engine/env-store-of (:env p))]\n        (cond-> (merge common {:site-pc (+ off pc -1) :pc (+ off pc)\n                               :frames (lower-frames lx seg pc (:env p))\n                               :regs regs :live (mapv first regs)\n                               :dest (phys seg (dec pc) (get-in p [:deliver :rd]))\n                               :resume-mode :write-result})\n          (some? store-of) (assoc :store-of store-of))))))\n\n\n(defn- layout-slice\n  \"`(mapv W (stack-layout (dec pc)))`, every member present in W.\"\n  [lx seg pc w]\n  (mapv (fn [x]\n          (when-not (contains? w x)\n            (refuse! :yin.k/undecodable :window-missing {:yin.k/pc pc :yin.k/register x}))\n          (positional-value lx (get w x)))\n        (stack-layout seg (dec pc))))\n\n\n(defn- stack-k\n  \"`[frames stack]`: each return frame's slice appended in order, its base\n   the cumulative length through its own slice (§4.3), `b(i) = b(i-1) +\n   |slice(i)|`.\"\n  [lx k]\n  (reduce (fn [[frames st] f]\n            (let [seg (payload-segment lx f)\n                  pc (:pc f)\n                  st (into st (layout-slice lx seg pc (:window f)))\n                  store-of (engine/env-store-of (:env f))]\n              [(conj frames (cond-> {:return-pc (+ (offset :stack (:kernel lx) seg) (a->h seg (dec pc)) 1)\n                                     :frames (lower-frames lx seg pc (:env f))\n                                     :stack-base (count st)}\n                              (some? store-of) (assoc :store-of store-of)))\n               st]))\n          [[] []]\n          k))\n\n\n(defn- lower-stack\n  [lx p fill]\n  (let [k (:kernel lx)\n        seg (payload-segment lx p)\n        off (offset :stack k seg)\n        [frames st] (stack-k lx (:k p))\n        common {:segment (:segment k) :format stack/format-tag :hash (:hash k) :image (:stack-id seg)\n                :continuation frames}]\n    (if (contains? p :site)\n      (cond-> (merge common {:pc (+ off (a->h seg (:site p)) 1) :resume-mode :return-result\n                             :stack st :frames (get fill :frames [])})\n        (some? (:store-of fill)) (assoc :store-of (:store-of fill)))\n      (let [pc (:pc p)\n            store-of (engine/env-store-of (:env p))]\n        (cond-> (merge common {:pc (+ off (a->h seg (dec pc)) 1)\n                               :frames (lower-frames lx seg pc (:env p))\n                               :stack (into st (layout-slice lx seg pc (:window p)))})\n          (some? store-of) (assoc :store-of store-of))))))\n\n\n(def ^:private canonical-keys\n  #{:segment :pc :site :env :window :k :deliver :type :id})\n\n\n(defn lower-state\n  \"Canonical act or tail payload `p` as the receiving kernel's entry\n   (§4.2, §4.3), engine fields carried. `fill` gives a lowered tail's\n   `:frames`/`:store-of` (default `[]`/nil), which the return transition\n   replaces unread (§5.6 item 8).\"\n  ([lx p] (lower-state lx p {}))\n  ([lx p fill]\n   (let [fields (apply dissoc p canonical-keys)]\n     (merge (cond-> fields (contains? fields :datom) (update :datom #(positional-value lx %)))\n            (if (= :stack (:kind lx)) (lower-stack lx p fill) (lower-register lx p fill))))))\n\n\n(defn lower-context\n  \"The lower context into kernel `k` over `space`; `address` maps a\n   payload's or closure's `:segment` to A (default: it is A).\"\n  ([k space] (lower-context k space identity))\n  ([k space address]\n   (assoc {} :kind (kernel-kind k) :kernel k :space space :free-env (or (:free-env k) {})\n          :owner (:owner k) :address address)))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Tasks\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- holds-procedure?\n  \"Whether kernel value `x` is or holds, through guest collections, a\n   closure or continuation.\"\n  [x]\n  (cond\n    (or (values/closure? x) (values/continuation? x)) true\n    (values/host-typed? x) false\n    (map? x) (boolean (some holds-procedure? (concat (keys x) (vals x))))\n    (coll? x) (boolean (some holds-procedure? x))\n    :else false))\n\n\n(defn canonical-task\n  \"Quiescent kernel `k` presented as semantic-register machine `template`\n   (a fresh machine of the same composition) for `rows/lift-task`: code\n   by A from `space`, every wait, parked record and value reconstructed\n   through the layout; resources, stores and counters carried. A running\n   kernel is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; install children are not carried\n   by this phase's layouts; a closure or continuation in the free\n   environment has no finite canonical form (§6.3).\"\n  [k space template]\n  (when (engine/active-continuation? k)\n    (refuse! :yin.k/not-at-safepoint nil {:yin.k/pc (:pc k)}))\n  (when (seq (:installs k))\n    (refuse! :yin.k/unsupported :installs {:yin.k/names (vec (keys (:installs k)))}))\n  (when-let [sym (some (fn [[sym x]] (when (holds-procedure? x) sym)) (sort-by (comp str key) (:free-env k)))]\n    (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :free-env-value {:yin.k/key sym}))\n  (let [cx (lift-context k space (:owner template))\n        conv #(canonical-value cx %)\n        parked (into {} (map (fn [[id rec]] [id (assoc (lift-state cx rec) :type :parked-continuation :id id)]))\n                     (:parked k))\n        value (:value k)\n        parked-value? (and (map? value) (= :parked-continuation (:type value)) (contains? parked (:id value)))\n        code (into {} (map (fn [[a s]] [a (assoc {} :vector (:vector s) :address a :analysis (:analysis s))]))\n                   (:segments space))]\n    (assoc template\n           :code code\n           :code-aliases (into {} (map (fn [a] [a a])) (keys code))\n           :program nil :control nil :window {} :k nil\n           :resources (:resources k)\n           :capability-secret (:capability-secret k)\n           :free-env (:free-env k)\n           :env (or (:free-env k) {})\n           :store (conv (:store k))\n           :module-stores (conv (or (:module-stores k) {}))\n           :parked parked\n           :wait-set (mapv #(lift-state cx %) (:wait-set k))\n           :ready-queue (:ready-queue k)\n           :value (if parked-value? (get parked (:id value)) (conv value))\n           :id-counter (:id-counter k)\n           :installs {}\n           :halted? (:halted? k)\n           :blocked? (:blocked? k))))\n\n\n(defn lift-task\n  \"§7.1 for a de Bruijn kernel: `rows/lift-task` over its canonical\n   presentation. `template` is a fresh semantic-register machine of the\n   kernel's composition; opts as rows/lift-task's.\"\n  [k space template serve! opts]\n  (rows/lift-task (canonical-task k space template) serve! opts))\n\n\n(defn attach-space\n  \"Kernel `k` holding the derived image of every segment of `space`, in\n   A order (§7.2: derive and attach the image for each A).\"\n  [k space]\n  (let [kind (kernel-kind k)]\n    (reduce (fn [k a]\n              (let [seg (segment-of space a)]\n                (if (= :stack kind)\n                  (stack/attach-image k (:stack seg) vm/stack-contract)\n                  (register/attach-image k (:register seg) vm/register-contract))))\n            k\n            (sort-by str (keys (:segments space))))))\n\n\n(defn machine-space\n  \"The space of the A vectors semantic-register machine `m` holds, and\n   the map from its segment ids to A.\"\n  [m]\n  [(space (map :vector (vals (:code m))))\n   (fn [seg] (or (get-in m [:code seg :address]) (when (contains? (:code-aliases m) seg) seg)))])\n\n\n(defn kernel-task\n  \"Step 12 for a de Bruijn receiver: semantic-register machine `m` (what\n   `rows/lower-task` restored) as fresh kernel `k` of the same\n   composition: every image derived and attached, the free environment\n   set, every wait, parked record and value lowered through the layout.\n   Delivers nothing, runs nothing.\"\n  [m k]\n  (let [[sp address] (machine-space m)\n        k (attach-space k sp)\n        lx (lower-context k sp address)\n        conv #(positional-value lx %)\n        parked (into {} (map (fn [[id rec]] [id (assoc (lower-state lx rec) :type :parked-continuation :id id)]))\n                     (:parked m))\n        value (:value m)\n        parked-value? (and (map? value) (= :parked-continuation (:type value)) (contains? parked (:id value)))]\n    (assoc k\n           :resources (:resources m)\n           :free-env (:free-env m)\n           :store (conv (:store m))\n           :module-stores (conv (or (:module-stores m) {}))\n           :parked parked\n           :wait-set (mapv #(lower-state lx %) (:wait-set m))\n           :ready-queue []\n           :value (if parked-value? (get parked (:id value)) (conv value))\n           :id-counter (:id-counter m)\n           :halted? (:halted? m)\n           :blocked? (:blocked? m))))\n\n\n(defn- lexical-names\n  \"The names the chain binds at `pc` of `v`.\"\n  [v pc]\n  (let [bodies (code/body-ranges v)]\n    (loop [b (code/body-index bodies pc) out #{}]\n      (if-let [owner (:owner (nth bodies b))]\n        (recur (code/body-index bodies owner) (into out (nth (nth v owner) 2)))\n        out))))\n\n\n(declare state-prim)\n\n\n(defn- value-prim\n  \"The path to the first `:prim` row carrier value `x` reaches as kernel\n   payload data: through guest collections, a closure's lexical bindings\n   (its positional frames) and a capture's state; or nil.\"\n  [code x path]\n  (when (and (vector? x) (= :row (first x)))\n    (let [n (second x) ss (:slots n)]\n      (case (:kind n)\n        :prim path\n        (:vec :list :set :map) (some (fn [[i y]] (value-prim code y (conj path i))) (map-indexed vector ss))\n        :closure (let [[a entry _ bindings] ss\n                       v (get code a)\n                       owner (when v (some (fn [pc]\n                                             (let [t (nth v pc)]\n                                               (when (and (= :closure (nth t 0)) (= entry (nth t 3))) pc)))\n                                           (range (count v))))\n                       names (if owner (lexical-names v owner) #{})]\n                   (some (fn [[s y]] (when (contains? names s) (value-prim code y (conj path s))))\n                         (partition 2 (:slots bindings))))\n        :reified (state-prim code (first ss) (conj path 0))\n        nil))))\n\n\n(defn- frame-prim\n  \"A frame's window and lexical bindings (§4.4: the free part is the\n   kernel's :free-env, never payload data).\"\n  [code a pc e w path]\n  (let [names (lexical-names (get code a) pc)]\n    (or (some (fn [[r y]] (value-prim code y (conj path :window r))) (partition 2 (:slots w)))\n        (some (fn [[sym y]] (when (contains? names sym) (value-prim code y (conj path :env sym))))\n              (partition 2 (:slots (first (:slots e))))))))\n\n\n(defn- state-prim\n  [code n path]\n  (let [kframes (fn [k]\n                  (some (fn [[i f]]\n                          (let [[a pc e w] (:slots f)]\n                            (frame-prim code a pc e w (conj path :k i))))\n                        (map-indexed vector (:slots k))))\n        [a pc e w k] (:slots n)]\n    (case (:kind n)\n      :act (or (frame-prim code a pc e w path) (kframes k))\n      :tail (kframes e)\n      nil)))\n\n\n(defn- row-procedure\n  \"The path to the first closure or continuation row carrier value `x`\n   holds, through guest collections; or nil.\"\n  [x path]\n  (when (and (vector? x) (= :row (first x)))\n    (let [n (second x)]\n      (case (:kind n)\n        (:closure :reified) path\n        (:vec :list :set :map) (some (fn [[i y]] (row-procedure y (conj path i))) (map-indexed vector (:slots n)))\n        nil))))\n\n\n(defn- free-env-procedure\n  \"§6.3: a de Bruijn kernel resolves free names through its one\n   `free-env` while a canonical closure snapshots its `env`, so a closure\n   or continuation held in the free environment has no finite canonical\n   form there. The path `[:free-env sym …]` to the first such value of\n   decoded task `task`, or nil.\"\n  [task]\n  (some (fn [[sym x]] (row-procedure x [:free-env sym]))\n        (partition 2 (:slots (nth (:slots task) 5)))))\n\n\n(defn- image-collision\n  \"§4.1: `[ident #{A …}]` for the first image of kind `kind` two carried A\n   derive, or nil. A de Bruijn kernel attaches one image once, so it\n   cannot hold both segments; no enumeration order selects an A. A\n   vector that derives no image is left to the carrier's own checks.\"\n  [code kind]\n  (let [ident (fn [a v]\n                (try\n                  (if (= :stack kind)\n                    (dcode/image-hash (:image (derive/stack-image v)))\n                    (rcode/register-hash (derive/register-image v)))\n                  (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _ [::underivable a])))\n        by-image (reduce (fn [acc [a v]] (update acc (ident a v) (fnil conj #{}) a)) {} code)]\n    (some (fn [[i as]] (when (< 1 (count as)) [i as])) (sort-by (comp str key) by-image))))\n\n\n(defn lower-defect\n  \"What a kernel of `kind` cannot hold, judged on decoded task `task`\n   before anything is attached: two carried A of one image (§4.1), install\n   children (not carried by this phase's layouts), a closure or\n   continuation in the free environment (§6.3), and for the register\n   kernel a primitive function in the payload data of a wait or parked\n   state (a window, a lexical binding, or reached through them), which\n   its restore refuses as `:continuation-registers` (`vm/machine-data?`).\n   nil when it can.\"\n  [task kind]\n  (let [slots (:slots task)\n        code (nth slots 11)\n        collision (image-collision code kind)\n        free (free-env-procedure task)]\n    (cond\n      collision\n      (assoc {} :yin.k/status :yin.k/unsupported :yin.k/kind :ambiguous-image\n             :yin.k/image (first collision) :yin.k/segments (second collision))\n\n      (seq (nth slots 12))\n      (assoc {} :yin.k/status :yin.k/unsupported :yin.k/kind :installs)\n\n      free\n      (assoc {} :yin.k/status :yin.k/unsupported :yin.k/kind :free-env-value :yin.k/path free)\n\n      (= :register kind)\n      (when-let [path (or (some (fn [[i w]] (state-prim code (first (:slots w)) [:frames i]))\n                                (map-indexed vector (nth slots 13)))\n                          (some (fn [[p st]] (state-prim code st [:parked p]))\n                                (partition 2 (:slots (nth slots 8)))))]\n        (assoc {} :yin.k/status :yin.k/unsupported :yin.k/kind :continuation-registers :yin.k/path path))\n\n      :else nil)))\n\n\n(defn lower-task\n  \"§7.2 into a de Bruijn kernel: decode `wire`, refuse what kernel `k`\n   cannot hold (`lower-defect`) before any attach, then `rows/lower-task`\n   into `scratch` (a fresh semantic-register machine of the same\n   composition: the carrier's steps 1-11 and restore) and `kernel-task`\n   into `k`. Throws the first refusal as ex-data.\"\n  ([scratch k wire attach!] (lower-task scratch k wire attach! {}))\n  ([scratch k wire attach! opts]\n   (let [task (rows/decode wire (merge rows/default-budget (:budget opts)))]\n     (when (= :task (:kind task))\n       (when-let [d (lower-defect task (kernel-kind k))]\n         (throw (ex-info \"Layout refused\" d))))\n     (kernel-task (rows/lower-task scratch wire attach! opts) k))))\n","numLines":964,"startLine":1,"totalLines":964}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Cd5CfRce4sHJLMpUmYrFdt","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
