{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GANQCtyWXC1g7duizprAaS","type":"tool_result","content":"2160\t                (make-woken-run-queue-entries v woken))))))\n2161\t\n2162\t\n2163\t(declare handle-effect resume-continuation)\n2164\t\n2165\t\n2166\t(defn run-loop\n2167\t  \"Generic eval loop with scheduler support. Ready entries are pure data;\n2168\t   `resume-fn` pops and restores them.\"\n2169\t  [state active? step-fn resume-fn]\n2170\t  (loop [v state]\n2171\t    (let [q (or (:ready-queue v) [])]\n2172\t      (cond (active? v) (recur (step-fn v))\n2173\t            (:blocked? v) (let [v' (check-wait-set v)]\n2174\t                            (if-let [resumed (resume-fn v')]\n2175\t                              (recur resumed)\n2176\t                              (telemetry/emit-snapshot v' :blocked)))\n2177\t            (seq q) (if-let [resumed (resume-fn v)]\n2178\t                      (recur resumed)\n2179\t                      v)\n2180\t            :else (if (:halted? v) (telemetry/emit-snapshot v :halt) v)))))\n2181\t\n2182\t\n2183\t(def ^:private waitset-diagnostics\n2184\t  \"The statuses `dao.stream.waitset/check` wakes an entry it could not poll\n2185\t   at all with: an unsupported reason, an unresolvable entry, or an answer —\n2186\t   or transport — it could not interpret. No park site in `yin.vm` produces\n2187\t   an entry that earns one, and an entry that earns one must not reach a\n2188\t   restore function as a value.\"\n2189\t  #{:dao.stream.waitset/unsupported-reason\n2190\t    :dao.stream.waitset/unresolved\n2191\t    :dao.stream.waitset/invalid-answer})\n2192\t\n2193\t\n2194\t(defn- terminal-resume-outcome\n2195\t  \"The outcome a woken entry resolved with, when it is one the immediate\n2196\t   path raises as an error. `handle-put` and `handle-next` raise exactly\n2197\t   the declared error outcomes of their operation, and whether the first\n2198\t   attempt blocked must not change that. An outcome outside this contract\n2199\t   version is the shaped refusal on the immediate path, so it is the\n2200\t   shaped refusal here too, never a raise. A waitset diagnostic is\n2201\t   terminal whatever the entry's `:reason` -- an unsupported reason is by\n2202\t   definition outside the `#{:next :put}` this check otherwise reads --\n2203\t   so it is named here rather than falling through to the restore. An\n2204\t   entry that was never polled carries no status.\"\n2205\t  [entry]\n2206\t  (let [status (:status entry)]\n2207\t    (cond\n2208\t      (contains? waitset-diagnostics status) status\n2209\t      (= :link-refused status) status\n2210\t      (contains? ffi/response-loss-statuses status) status\n2211\t      (contains? #{:next :put} (:reason entry))\n2212\t      (when (contains? (get wake-error-outcomes (:reason entry)) status)\n2213\t        status))))\n2214\t\n2215\t\n2216\t(defn- throw-terminal-resume!\n2217\t  \"Fail a resumed entry exactly as the immediate operation would have. A\n2218\t   waitset diagnostic names its status and the entry that earned it: there\n2219\t   is no stream operation whose error vocabulary it belongs to.\"\n2220\t  [entry o]\n2221\t  (cond\n2222\t    (contains? waitset-diagnostics o)\n2223\t    (fail \"Wait-set entry woke with a diagnostic\" {:status o, :entry entry})\n2224\t    ;; a refused or lost link, or a refused install: the effect's error\n2225\t    (= :link-refused o)\n2226\t    (fail (str \"Module link refused: \"\n2227\t               (clojure.core/name (or (:reason (:refusal entry)) :refused)))\n2228\t          (assoc (:refusal entry) :link-module (:name entry)))\n2229\t    ;; an FFI response stream that can no longer answer this call\n2230\t    (contains? ffi/response-loss-statuses o)\n2231\t    (ffi/throw-response-lost! (ffi/response-call-id entry) o)\n2232\t    (= :next (:reason entry))\n2233\t    (fail \"Stream read failed\"\n2234\t          {:outcome o,\n2235\t           :stream-id (:stream-id entry),\n2236\t           :cursor-id (:id (:cursor-ref entry))})\n2237\t    :else (fail \"Stream append failed\"\n2238\t                {:outcome o, :stream-id (:stream-id entry)})))\n2239\t\n2240\t\n2241\t(defn resume-from-run-queue\n2242\t  \"Pop first entry from the ready-queue, merge its resource updates into\n2243\t   the private `:resources` table, and restore VM-specific context.\n2244\t\n2245\t   Restoration is dispatched here rather than carried on the entry, so a\n2246\t   ready entry holds registers, ids, and values — never a closure. The\n2247\t   terminal-outcome check runs before the restore: a woken retry that ended\n2248\t   in an outcome the immediate operation raises as an error must fail the\n2249\t   same way here, not reach Yin code as a value.\n2250\t\n2251\t   `restore-fn` is called with three arguments, `base entry val`, where\n2252\t   `val` is the woken `:value` — the same signature `resume-continuation`\n2253\t   uses, so one restore serves both engine call sites\n2254\t   (`yin.vm.engine.md` §7, edit 2).\"\n2255\t  [state restore-fn]\n2256\t  (let [run-queue (or (:ready-queue state) [])]\n2257\t    (when (seq run-queue)\n2258\t      (let [entry (first run-queue)\n2259\t            rest-queue (subvec run-queue 1)\n2260\t            updates (:resource-updates entry)\n2261\t            ;; a woken entry only ever carries engine-minted keyword keys\n2262\t            ;; (cursor ids)\n2263\t            _ (when-let [k (some #(when-not (keyword? %) %) (keys updates))]\n2264\t                (fail \"Ready entry resource update carries a non-minted key\"\n2265\t                      {:rule :resource-update-key, :key k}))\n2266\t            base (assoc state\n2267\t                        :ready-queue rest-queue\n2268\t                        :resources (merge (:resources state) updates)\n2269\t                        :blocked? false\n2270\t                        :halted? false)]\n2271\t        (if-let [terminal (terminal-resume-outcome entry)]\n2272\t          (throw-terminal-resume! entry terminal)\n2273\t          (restore-fn base entry (:value entry)))))))\n2274\t\n2275\t\n2276\t(defn scheduler-round\n2277\t  \"One round between continuations: poll the wait set, then resume whatever\n2278\t   woke, through `restore-fn` (`base entry val -> state`, the one restore\n2279\t   signature `yin.vm.engine.md` §2.1 states). Returns the polled state when\n2280\t   nothing woke, so a caller always gets a state back.\n2281\t\n2282\t   This is `yin.vm.semantic/scheduler-round` with its restore made a\n2283\t   parameter (`yin.vm.engine.md` §7, edit 1); every VM was writing this\n2284\t   composition by hand.\"\n2285\t  [state restore-fn]\n2286\t  (let [v' (check-wait-set state)]\n2287\t    (or (resume-from-run-queue v' restore-fn) v')))\n2288\t\n2289\t\n2290\t(defn park-id\n2291\t  \"The local park id the next `park-continuation` mints: `:parked-N` from\n2292\t   this VM's own counter, unique within the VM only.\"\n2293\t  [state]\n2294\t  (keyword (str \"parked-\" (or (:id-counter state) 0))))\n2295\t\n2296\t\n2297\t(defn park-continuation\n2298\t  \"Add a parked continuation entry and halt the VM.\n2299\t\n2300\t   With `id`, that id is both the `:parked` map key and the entry's `:id`\n2301\t   -- an FFI call parks under its call id (`yin.vm.ffi/call-id`), so one\n2302\t   id is retained and correlated, never a second mapping. The counter\n2303\t   advances either way, so the local half of the next id stays fresh.\"\n2304\t  ([state cont-fields] (park-continuation state cont-fields (park-id state)))\n2305\t  ([state cont-fields id]\n2306\t   (let [id-counter (or (:id-counter state) 0)\n2307\t         parked (merge {:type :parked-continuation, :id id} cont-fields)]\n2308\t     (-> state\n2309\t         (update :parked assoc id parked)\n2310\t         (assoc :value parked\n2311\t                :halted? true\n2312\t                :id-counter (inc id-counter))\n2313\t         (telemetry/emit-snapshot :park {:parked-id id})))))\n2314\t\n2315\t\n2316\t(defn resume-continuation\n2317\t  \"Restore state from a parked continuation. A machine whose custody\n2318\t   gate is `:exporting` or `:ended` refuses a direct resume.\"\n2319\t  [state parked-id resume-val restore-fn]\n2320\t  (gate-refuse! state {:effect :resume})\n2321\t  (if-let [parked (get-in state [:parked parked-id])]\n2322\t    (let [new-state (update state :parked dissoc parked-id)]\n2323\t      (-> (restore-fn new-state parked resume-val)\n2324\t          (telemetry/emit-snapshot :resume {:parked-id parked-id})))\n2325\t    (fail \"Cannot resume: parked continuation not found\"\n2326\t          {:parked-id parked-id})))\n2327\t\n2328\t\n2329\t(defn gensym\n2330\t  \"Generate a unique ID and return [id updated-state].\"\n2331\t  ([state] (gensym state \"id\"))\n2332\t  ([state prefix]\n2333\t   (let [id-counter (or (:id-counter state) 0)\n2334\t         id (gen-id prefix id-counter)]\n2335\t     [id (assoc state :id-counter (inc id-counter))])))\n2336\t\n2337\t\n2338\t(defn handle-stream-block\n2339\t  [result entry]\n2340\t  (if (:park result)\n2341\t    (let [entry (or entry\n2342\t                    (fail \"Parked entry required for blocking stream\"\n2343\t                          {:result result}))\n2344\t          new-state (-> (:state result)\n2345\t                        (update :wait-set (fnil conj []) entry)\n2346\t                        (assoc :value :yin/blocked\n2347\t                               :blocked? true\n2348\t                               :halted? false))]\n2349\t      {:state new-state, :value :yin/blocked, :blocked? true})\n2350\t    {:state (:state result), :value (:value result), :blocked? false}))\n2351\t\n2352\t\n2353\t(defn- with-primitive-effects\n2354\t  \"`state` whose `:callable-effects` index was built from its current\n2355\t   `:primitives` map: the held index when that map is `identical?` to\n2356\t   the one it was built from, else a rebuilt one.\"\n2357\t  [state]\n2358\t  (let [primitives (:primitives state)]\n2359\t    (if (identical? primitives (:primitives (:callable-effects state)))\n2360\t      state\n2361\t      (assoc state\n2362\t             :callable-effects\n2363\t             (vm/primitive-effects-index primitives\n2364\t                                         (:primitive-profiles state))))))\n2365\t\n2366\t\n2367\t(defn check-callee-effect!\n2368\t  \"Refuse `effect`, the result of applying host callable `f`, when `f`\n2369\t   carries a profile whose declared effect set lacks the effect's kind\n2370\t   (D4, layered on the host type). `f`'s declared set is the union of\n2371\t   its primitive and module declarations, read from identity-keyed\n2372\t   indexes: the VM's `:callable-effects`, rebuilt here when `:primitives`\n2373\t   changed since it was built, and the registry's, which\n2374\t   `module/register-host-module` maintains. The check runs only on an\n2375\t   effect result and costs nothing on an ordinary call. A callable with\n2376\t   no profile is the composition's trusted value and passes. Returns\n2377\t   `state`, carrying the index it checked against, for the caller to\n2378\t   dispatch the effect on.\"\n2379\t  [state f effect]\n2380\t  (let [state (with-primitive-effects state)\n2381\t        from-primitives (get (:index (:callable-effects state)) f)\n2382\t        from-modules (get (:callable-effects (:modules state)) f)\n2383\t        declared (if (and from-primitives from-modules)\n2384\t                   (into from-primitives from-modules)\n2385\t                   (or from-primitives from-modules))]\n2386\t    (if (and declared (not (contains? declared (:effect effect))))\n2387\t      (fail \"Effect outside the callee's declared profile\"\n2388\t            {:yin.k/status :yin.k/undeclared-effect,\n2389\t             :yin.k/effect (:effect effect),"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f7716a8d-bcb9-4b49-9ad0-ed6a5daed388","uuid":"a60c7c76-3a63-4aa8-8f56-a009b86a5b3c","timestamp":"2026-10-13T09:47:57.767Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7plan/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc","content":"                (make-woken-run-queue-entries v woken))))))\n\n\n(declare handle-effect resume-continuation)\n\n\n(defn run-loop\n  \"Generic eval loop with scheduler support. Ready entries are pure data;\n   `resume-fn` pops and restores them.\"\n  [state active? step-fn resume-fn]\n  (loop [v state]\n    (let [q (or (:ready-queue v) [])]\n      (cond (active? v) (recur (step-fn v))\n            (:blocked? v) (let [v' (check-wait-set v)]\n                            (if-let [resumed (resume-fn v')]\n                              (recur resumed)\n                              (telemetry/emit-snapshot v' :blocked)))\n            (seq q) (if-let [resumed (resume-fn v)]\n                      (recur resumed)\n                      v)\n            :else (if (:halted? v) (telemetry/emit-snapshot v :halt) v)))))\n\n\n(def ^:private waitset-diagnostics\n  \"The statuses `dao.stream.waitset/check` wakes an entry it could not poll\n   at all with: an unsupported reason, an unresolvable entry, or an answer —\n   or transport — it could not interpret. No park site in `yin.vm` produces\n   an entry that earns one, and an entry that earns one must not reach a\n   restore function as a value.\"\n  #{:dao.stream.waitset/unsupported-reason\n    :dao.stream.waitset/unresolved\n    :dao.stream.waitset/invalid-answer})\n\n\n(defn- terminal-resume-outcome\n  \"The outcome a woken entry resolved with, when it is one the immediate\n   path raises as an error. `handle-put` and `handle-next` raise exactly\n   the declared error outcomes of their operation, and whether the first\n   attempt blocked must not change that. An outcome outside this contract\n   version is the shaped refusal on the immediate path, so it is the\n   shaped refusal here too, never a raise. A waitset diagnostic is\n   terminal whatever the entry's `:reason` -- an unsupported reason is by\n   definition outside the `#{:next :put}` this check otherwise reads --\n   so it is named here rather than falling through to the restore. An\n   entry that was never polled carries no status.\"\n  [entry]\n  (let [status (:status entry)]\n    (cond\n      (contains? waitset-diagnostics status) status\n      (= :link-refused status) status\n      (contains? ffi/response-loss-statuses status) status\n      (contains? #{:next :put} (:reason entry))\n      (when (contains? (get wake-error-outcomes (:reason entry)) status)\n        status))))\n\n\n(defn- throw-terminal-resume!\n  \"Fail a resumed entry exactly as the immediate operation would have. A\n   waitset diagnostic names its status and the entry that earned it: there\n   is no stream operation whose error vocabulary it belongs to.\"\n  [entry o]\n  (cond\n    (contains? waitset-diagnostics o)\n    (fail \"Wait-set entry woke with a diagnostic\" {:status o, :entry entry})\n    ;; a refused or lost link, or a refused install: the effect's error\n    (= :link-refused o)\n    (fail (str \"Module link refused: \"\n               (clojure.core/name (or (:reason (:refusal entry)) :refused)))\n          (assoc (:refusal entry) :link-module (:name entry)))\n    ;; an FFI response stream that can no longer answer this call\n    (contains? ffi/response-loss-statuses o)\n    (ffi/throw-response-lost! (ffi/response-call-id entry) o)\n    (= :next (:reason entry))\n    (fail \"Stream read failed\"\n          {:outcome o,\n           :stream-id (:stream-id entry),\n           :cursor-id (:id (:cursor-ref entry))})\n    :else (fail \"Stream append failed\"\n                {:outcome o, :stream-id (:stream-id entry)})))\n\n\n(defn resume-from-run-queue\n  \"Pop first entry from the ready-queue, merge its resource updates into\n   the private `:resources` table, and restore VM-specific context.\n\n   Restoration is dispatched here rather than carried on the entry, so a\n   ready entry holds registers, ids, and values — never a closure. The\n   terminal-outcome check runs before the restore: a woken retry that ended\n   in an outcome the immediate operation raises as an error must fail the\n   same way here, not reach Yin code as a value.\n\n   `restore-fn` is called with three arguments, `base entry val`, where\n   `val` is the woken `:value` — the same signature `resume-continuation`\n   uses, so one restore serves both engine call sites\n   (`yin.vm.engine.md` §7, edit 2).\"\n  [state restore-fn]\n  (let [run-queue (or (:ready-queue state) [])]\n    (when (seq run-queue)\n      (let [entry (first run-queue)\n            rest-queue (subvec run-queue 1)\n            updates (:resource-updates entry)\n            ;; a woken entry only ever carries engine-minted keyword keys\n            ;; (cursor ids)\n            _ (when-let [k (some #(when-not (keyword? %) %) (keys updates))]\n                (fail \"Ready entry resource update carries a non-minted key\"\n                      {:rule :resource-update-key, :key k}))\n            base (assoc state\n                        :ready-queue rest-queue\n                        :resources (merge (:resources state) updates)\n                        :blocked? false\n                        :halted? false)]\n        (if-let [terminal (terminal-resume-outcome entry)]\n          (throw-terminal-resume! entry terminal)\n          (restore-fn base entry (:value entry)))))))\n\n\n(defn scheduler-round\n  \"One round between continuations: poll the wait set, then resume whatever\n   woke, through `restore-fn` (`base entry val -> state`, the one restore\n   signature `yin.vm.engine.md` §2.1 states). Returns the polled state when\n   nothing woke, so a caller always gets a state back.\n\n   This is `yin.vm.semantic/scheduler-round` with its restore made a\n   parameter (`yin.vm.engine.md` §7, edit 1); every VM was writing this\n   composition by hand.\"\n  [state restore-fn]\n  (let [v' (check-wait-set state)]\n    (or (resume-from-run-queue v' restore-fn) v')))\n\n\n(defn park-id\n  \"The local park id the next `park-continuation` mints: `:parked-N` from\n   this VM's own counter, unique within the VM only.\"\n  [state]\n  (keyword (str \"parked-\" (or (:id-counter state) 0))))\n\n\n(defn park-continuation\n  \"Add a parked continuation entry and halt the VM.\n\n   With `id`, that id is both the `:parked` map key and the entry's `:id`\n   -- an FFI call parks under its call id (`yin.vm.ffi/call-id`), so one\n   id is retained and correlated, never a second mapping. The counter\n   advances either way, so the local half of the next id stays fresh.\"\n  ([state cont-fields] (park-continuation state cont-fields (park-id state)))\n  ([state cont-fields id]\n   (let [id-counter (or (:id-counter state) 0)\n         parked (merge {:type :parked-continuation, :id id} cont-fields)]\n     (-> state\n         (update :parked assoc id parked)\n         (assoc :value parked\n                :halted? true\n                :id-counter (inc id-counter))\n         (telemetry/emit-snapshot :park {:parked-id id})))))\n\n\n(defn resume-continuation\n  \"Restore state from a parked continuation. A machine whose custody\n   gate is `:exporting` or `:ended` refuses a direct resume.\"\n  [state parked-id resume-val restore-fn]\n  (gate-refuse! state {:effect :resume})\n  (if-let [parked (get-in state [:parked parked-id])]\n    (let [new-state (update state :parked dissoc parked-id)]\n      (-> (restore-fn new-state parked resume-val)\n          (telemetry/emit-snapshot :resume {:parked-id parked-id})))\n    (fail \"Cannot resume: parked continuation not found\"\n          {:parked-id parked-id})))\n\n\n(defn gensym\n  \"Generate a unique ID and return [id updated-state].\"\n  ([state] (gensym state \"id\"))\n  ([state prefix]\n   (let [id-counter (or (:id-counter state) 0)\n         id (gen-id prefix id-counter)]\n     [id (assoc state :id-counter (inc id-counter))])))\n\n\n(defn handle-stream-block\n  [result entry]\n  (if (:park result)\n    (let [entry (or entry\n                    (fail \"Parked entry required for blocking stream\"\n                          {:result result}))\n          new-state (-> (:state result)\n                        (update :wait-set (fnil conj []) entry)\n                        (assoc :value :yin/blocked\n                               :blocked? true\n                               :halted? false))]\n      {:state new-state, :value :yin/blocked, :blocked? true})\n    {:state (:state result), :value (:value result), :blocked? false}))\n\n\n(defn- with-primitive-effects\n  \"`state` whose `:callable-effects` index was built from its current\n   `:primitives` map: the held index when that map is `identical?` to\n   the one it was built from, else a rebuilt one.\"\n  [state]\n  (let [primitives (:primitives state)]\n    (if (identical? primitives (:primitives (:callable-effects state)))\n      state\n      (assoc state\n             :callable-effects\n             (vm/primitive-effects-index primitives\n                                         (:primitive-profiles state))))))\n\n\n(defn check-callee-effect!\n  \"Refuse `effect`, the result of applying host callable `f`, when `f`\n   carries a profile whose declared effect set lacks the effect's kind\n   (D4, layered on the host type). `f`'s declared set is the union of\n   its primitive and module declarations, read from identity-keyed\n   indexes: the VM's `:callable-effects`, rebuilt here when `:primitives`\n   changed since it was built, and the registry's, which\n   `module/register-host-module` maintains. The check runs only on an\n   effect result and costs nothing on an ordinary call. A callable with\n   no profile is the composition's trusted value and passes. Returns\n   `state`, carrying the index it checked against, for the caller to\n   dispatch the effect on.\"\n  [state f effect]\n  (let [state (with-primitive-effects state)\n        from-primitives (get (:index (:callable-effects state)) f)\n        from-modules (get (:callable-effects (:modules state)) f)\n        declared (if (and from-primitives from-modules)\n                   (into from-primitives from-modules)\n                   (or from-primitives from-modules))]\n    (if (and declared (not (contains? declared (:effect effect))))\n      (fail \"Effect outside the callee's declared profile\"\n            {:yin.k/status :yin.k/undeclared-effect,\n             :yin.k/effect (:effect effect),","numLines":230,"startLine":2160,"totalLines":2789}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01GANQCtyWXC1g7duizprAaS","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
